/** Translation maps for siman 048 */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  "mechaber": {
    "1#main": `The laws of treifot in the kivah and kereis. In it are 12 seifim. Kivah (kivah is the pouch in the animal's innards into which food enters the kereis; at the end of the kereis called paunch it is shaped like a hat and is called beit hakosot, and food enters from beit hakosot to the meses and from the meses to the kivah and from the kivah to the dekin) — if punctured, treifah. And if the chelev on the yeter seals the puncture — kosher; but that on the arch does not seal:`,
    "10#main": `That which we said "from one side — kosher" means from inside toward the stomach; but if the side outside faces the cavity of the body — even if it did not puncture at all, but a needle is found in the cavity of the body — treifah:`,
    "11#main": `A needle found in the kurkavan and all of it is swallowed in the thickness of its flesh and half a finger-width remains on the side of the pouch — also on the outside side, so that the needle was not eating and no puncture was recognizable neither toward inside nor toward outside — we suspend to be lenient: {Rama: For the needle comes from inside and it is like punctured from one side; and if there is no korat dam from outside — kosher (Rashba). And some are stringent and forbid even in such a case (Hagahot Shaarei Dura in name of Maharam and Orach Chaim haArukh). And one should be concerned for their words if not in great loss:}`,
    "12#main": `A minor who found a needle stuck in the kereis — one should be stringent and forbid on his word if he is sharp to know and to aim in these matters:`,
    "2#main": `Kereis — if punctured, treifah; and it has nothing to seal it, for the chelev on it is forbidden: {Rama: Worms found in the kereis — kosher, for they grew from the secretion or the animal ate them. (Shaarei Dura siman 47):}`,
    "3#main": `Flesh covering most of the kereis — and this is the place from the belly — if when torn the kereis would emerge — if this flesh was torn, treifah even though this tear did not reach the kereis until it is visible; rather, since most of the thickness of this flesh was torn or removed — treifah. And how much is the measure of this tear in its length — a length of a tefach; and if it was a small animal and most of the length of the flesh covering the kereis was torn — even though the length of the tear is not a tefach — treifah, since most of it was torn:`,
    "4#main": `If this flesh was bored circularly or in length — if there was missing more than a sela — and it is so that three date pits can enter one next to the other with difficulty if food remains around it when they eat it, or loosely when there is no food at all — behold this is treifah, for if this tear is stretched it will stand at a tefach:`,
    "5#main": `If this flesh dissolved until the doctor scrapes it — it is considered as removed: {Rama: And we are not expert in flesh that the doctor scrapes; therefore wherever the appearance of the flesh changed — its law is as removed (Mordechai and Beit Yosef in name of Hagahot Maimoniyot):}`,
    "6#main": `A needle found in the cavity of the kivah or kereis — its law is like a needle found in beit hakosot:`,
    "7#main": `The meses and beit hakosot are attached to one another; and if they were punctured in the place of their attachment — even if the puncture is mafpolash from one to the other — kosher; and if the puncture is on other sides that are not attached together — if the puncture is mafpolash from side to side — treifah; and if not — kosher, whether in the meses or in beit hakosot: {Rama: And some forbid in the meses even without a mafpolash puncture (Tur in name of Rashi and Orach Chaim kelal 165 and Tur haArukh siman 165); and so is the practice unless in a place of great loss, for then one may rely on those who permit:}`,
    "8#main": `If in one of them a needle or thorn is stuck and it did not puncture from side to side — they turn it over and examine it from outside; if korat dam is found on it — it is known that it was entirely punctured before slaughter and is treifah; and if not — kosher (Tosafot and Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 6 in name of Semag and all poskim) (even if only a bit of the outer skin did not puncture) (see s.k. 21). And this is when they did not salt it or rinse it — even if its point is toward the outside: {Rama: And some are lenient to permit if it did not puncture from side to side and was rinsed and salted, or was lost and not examined from outside whether korat dam was on it (so is implied in Hagahot Semak and Rambam and Ra'ash below, and several poskim) — and one may rely on them in a place of great loss.} And if the needle went from side to side while still stuck in it — they examine it whether korat dam is found around the needle (and all the more on it) (Mordechai and poskim — and this is Shulchan Aruch Orach Chaim laws of forbidden foods daf 51 and Ketuvot daf 76) — it is known that before slaughter it was entirely punctured and is treifah; and if not — after slaughter it was forced through and is kosher, if they did not salt or rinse it; for since the needle is still there, if it had passed while alive there would be blood around it. But when the needle is not before us — we forbid as if it was entirely punctured even if there is no korat dam: {Rama: Wherever examination is required — if they did not examine, such as if it was lost or they took the needle from there — treifah, for it is as if they rinsed and salted it (Rashba siman 360). Some say that in our time, since we are not expert in examinations — one should declare treifah if the needle punctured from both sides even if no korat dam or rust was found, for we do not rely on our examination once it punctured from both sides (R' Yehudah Zahav and Orach Chaim haArukh); and so is the practice. A needle or thorn found in the meses and beit hakosot in their cavity and not stuck in them at all — kosher in every case, and no examination is required (Hagahot Maimoniyot). And some are lenient that even if the needle punctured from one side — one need not examine from outside if there is korat dam on it (Rambam and Ra'ash) — and one may rely on them if the loss is great and examination is impossible, such as if it was salted or rinsed or lost:}`,
    "9#main": `If rust is found on it — its law is like korat dam found on it: {Rama: And likewise if rust is found from outside opposite the needle — if it punctured from one side it is treifah as if korat dam were found there (Beit Yosef):}`,
  },
  "turei-zahav": {
    "1#_": `On the yeter. Explanation: the kivah is shaped like a kind of arch; chelev on it from outside is called dakashta, and chelev inside the circle is called yeter — meaning chelev in the extra place on the arch. And even though we rule in siman 64 on chelev on the yeter that it is forbidden, and we rule that tame chelev does not seal — the reason it seals here is because we say in the Gemara that the people of Eretz Yisrael eat that on the yeter, only that the people of Bavel practiced to forbid it; nevertheless it helps also for the people of Bavel who permit through sealing the puncture. But that on the arch is forbidden to eat for everyone — therefore it does not seal.`,
    "11#א": `We suspend to be lenient. Since in any case there was a puncture on the outside side or inside and the puncture was sealed — we suspend to say it came from inside, since its way is to come from the esophagus; and further we established it on presumption of kashrut that it had — so Beit Yosef in name of Rashba.`,
    "11#ב": `And some are stringent and forbid even in such a case, etc. The reason: we are concerned lest the needle came from outside and punctured other organs; and if it appears to come from inside to outside and punctured entirely and there is no korat dam at all — Darkei Moshe wrote treifah; for we do not require korat dam except in the meses and beit hakosot whose wall is thin — one may say that after slaughter a small thing such as this was punctured; but kurkavan flesh is thick — we do not say lest it punctured in such a short time, and we do not suspend to say perhaps it punctured from life all the flesh except a minority and that minority was punctured after slaughter — we do not say that. And since in any case it must be that most was punctured from life, we suspend the minority after the majority likewise. So is implied from Tur's language: specifically regarding punctured inside and not outside is kurkavan law like meses and beit hakosot; but here where it punctured entirely regarding korat dam they are not equal. Therefore the practice every day is to declare treifah when punctured from both sides even if there is no korat dam outside. I wondered on Mahari Chaim z"l who wrote at end of siman 49 that also regarding korat dam when punctured from both sides kurkavan is like meses and beit hakosot — and he wrote it is simple; he did not see these words of Darkei Moshe.`,
    "11#ג": `If not in great loss. That which he ruled leniently is because it is more plausible that it came from inside through the esophagus, for such is the way of swallowing there, as Rashba brought at the beginning of this seif; and even though he wrote there another reason — that we established it on presumption of kashrut — it appears that reason alone is not sufficient to permit by itself; for otherwise there is difficulty from what Rashba himself wrote that Rama copied in seif 8: wherever examination is required and it was lost or they took the needle — forbidden; and we do not say we establish on its presumption — but certainly he did not write so here except combined with the first reason. And there is a difference in every case where there is doubt whether the kurkavan was also punctured from the outside and examination is impossible — such as lost after a needle was found in it, or cut in a way examination is impossible — one forbids from safek for the reason mentioned, since examination is required and impossible; and even though in responsum of R' Moshe Mintz siman 66 it appears his view leans to leniency in such a case from "shechutah hutrah" — it does not appear to me to rely on this; and so my brother Mahari z"l in a responsum regarding a child who felt in his mouth while eating that there is a needle in the kurkavan and cried — and they saw at the place of the tooth's bite in the kurkavan the needle emerged partly from there — and he forbade everything, even mixtures, because the chicken piece was worthy of honor even if it is weak; and the law is with him that even though the treifah is safek here, nevertheless what that the honorable piece is derabbanan does not help when there is safek derabbanan, as I wrote in name of Orach Chaim siman 100; and so is clear in Hagahot Shaarei Dura and Orach Chaim haArukh in name of Maharam regarding a needle in kurkavan that Rama mentioned here — some are stringent and forbid all mixtures; and even though Rama ruled not like those stringent — nevertheless in this safek where there is no reason for permit more than certain prohibition, we rule thus: also mixtures are forbidden whenever the prohibition is an honorable piece.`,
    "12#_": `A minor in whom a needle was found, etc. It is a simple matter that a gentile is not believed at all in this — a minor is preferable to a gentile, as stated in Choshen Mishpat siman 35 seif 7: a minor and an adult are preferable to a gentile who converted. And I saw in a responsum to Maharam of Lublin siman 66 regarding a gentile who did melacha with an Israelite in flaying the animal and removing the innards — he came to the Israelite with a nail in his hand and said: see this iron nail that I found and it was stuck in the animal's kereis; the Jew said: where was it stuck, and he showed the place stuck inside the kereis shaped like peels in a manner the animal would not have been treifah if so; and he ruled not to rely on his words to permit but to forbid — for if the Israelite had come before the gentile removed the nail, it would have required examination whether the needle punctured outward; and now examination is impossible — forbidden from safek; and he elaborated there on being stringent in safek when not examined. I do not believe this ruling came from the Gaon's mouth — for certainly there is no treifah concern; if you do not believe him regarding the place of the nail's sticking, you also do not believe him regarding finding the needle at all — the mouth that forbade is the mouth that permitted, as stated in mishnah chapter 6 of Demai: one who sells produce in Syria and says they are from Eretz Yisrael — he must tithe (explanation: demai); if he says they are tithed — he is believed, for the mouth that forbade is the mouth that permitted — meaning: just as we believe him that they are from Eretz Yisrael, so we believe him that they are tithed; and likewise here. And greater: it appears even if gentiles do not show the place of sticking — such as when they cannot show — one does not forbid in any manner; for even regarding a minor we say here he is not believed to forbid unless he is sharp and knows to aim in the matter; and so below siman 127; all the more a gentile is not believed to forbid at all. And even regarding an adult Israelite we say there he is not believed unless it is in his hand the first time, as stated there — then there is credibility for an Israelite; but a gentile does not enter this category at all. But one may say: if the gentile is believed that he says he found a needle in the kereis somewhat inside the wall of the kereis — it is possible it was also punctured mafpolash, only the gentile did not know to be precise — then one forbids from safek; and proof: Beit Yosef wrote siman 16 in name of Rashba regarding oto v'et beno — if the gentile is believed to him, likewise here. And likewise regarding whether the vessel is ben yomo as siman 123 seif 16 — only when speaking offhandedly; but if the gentile said he found it stuck along the length of the nail inside the folds of the meses called heib — like small chambers in a manner the point of the needle is not opposite the thickness of the wall — one does not forbid even if the gentile is believed, and even if an Israelite said he found so and the kereis is not before us — we do not forbid from safek; for even according to what I wrote above to forbid without examination if punctured from one side — that is when the needle entered its point into the thickness of the wall and there is concern it went further and punctured; not so when lying along inside the skins of small chambers — no treifah concern unless the kereis is before us to examine where possible; and where impossible — one does not forbid from safek in this, as it appears clear and correct.`,
    "2#א": `Kereis that was punctured, etc., and it has nothing to seal it, etc. This is Rambam's language, and it is difficult — for we find proper sealing in it, namely if it was punctured in the place where the spleen is attached to it and the spleen seals it, as Rambam himself writes in that chapter: "And likewise any puncture that flesh or chelev permitted in eating seals — it is permitted." If so, here too the flesh of the spleen properly seals the puncture. And one may say: since there is a membrane on the spleen that is forbidden as full chelev in the place attached to the kereis, as written siman 64 seif 10 — "membrane on the teat of the spleen one is liable for it," etc., and this is where it is attached to the kereis; and so Rashba wrote in chapter 3 siman 57: "that on the teat is attached there to the kereis and to full chelev," end of his words — therefore there is no sealing for kereis punctured, for tame chelev does not seal; and even in the thin place of the spleen there is in any case a prohibition of chelev and it does not seal at all. And see what I wrote siman 43 seif 1 further on this; and so it appears simple, since Rambam wrote anonymously "you have nothing to seal" — it implies in every place. And greater than this: Hagahot Shaarei Dura — spleen punctured, the kereis does not seal it.`,
    "2#ב": `Worms found in the kereis. Explanation: they are placed inside; and if they go outside and it is not known whether they left before slaughter — it appears their law is like a healthy animal in siman 36 seif 5.`,
    "3#א": `If it tears, etc. All this deals when the tear was made by illness; but if made by a thorn — treifah in any case in mafpolash even in less than a tefach, lest the intestines were punctured, as Rashba wrote in responsum siman 287, and so Orach Chaim haArukh kelal 91 for us who are not expert in examination. And so Darkei Moshe siman 91 regarding made by a thorn.`,
    "3#ב": `Most of the thickness of the flesh. This is Rambam's view; and his language is that which the Gemara says regarding the outer kereis "in its majority" — meaning in the majority of its thickness; and even if not mafpolash it is treifah. Rabbenu Yerucham wondered on him: if so we would not know whether it was punctured mafpolash — how is its law, since the Gemara does not deal with this. Beit Yosef wrote: and to me it appears that because we taught anonymously, etc. — explanation of his words: it is simple that if torn with a knife it is forbidden even in any amount in mafpolash from concern lest the kereis was punctured; and since the tanna did not distinguish between tear by knife or by illness — we must say that also in illness it was in something; and why did the tanna say we require a majority — but certainly the tanna deals with not mafpolash, and there is truly no distinction between illness and knife — in both we require a majority; in mafpolash treifah in both in any amount. And what Beit Yosef wrote "their measures are equal" means whether by illness or by knife they are equal — in mafpolash in any amount, and in not mafpolash in its majority. This is explanation of his words; but they are very astonishing — for certainly there is a distinction between knife and illness in mafpolash, only that the knife prohibition does not apply specifically here; for in every place facing the cavity of the body there is concern in puncture by knife or thorn lest it touched inner organs and punctured them — why should the tanna distinguish here in flesh covering the kereis between knife and illness, when the knife stringency does not depend specifically here. And further: in esophagus there is a distinction between thorn and illness as stated siman 33 in Shulchan Aruch from Tosafot and Ra'ash — and so we rule; and why not distinguish here too. And the tanna need not mention this distinction — for knife stringency is not from itself but from concern lest the kereis was punctured, and the tanna does not deal with that but with treifah from itself. And more difficult for his view: why did the tanna mention treifah that is not mafpolash which is in a majority, and not treifah that is mafpolash which is in any amount — there is a great innovation that it is forbidden even from illness. But it is certain there is no prohibition here even in mafpolash in less than the measure of three pits — namely if by illness; but by thorn or knife we are concerned in every place facing the cavity of the body as written siman 91. Also Rashba challenged Rambam as Beit Yosef brought, and he holds that specifically mafpolash we require in the measures mentioned here; and Rashba brought for halacha like them. Nevertheless one may not be lenient since they established in Shulchan Aruch for halacha. Nevertheless it is clear that also regarding mafpolash its law is thus — that we forbid only at a tefach, as written here regarding thickness, since others explained so regarding mafpolash as above.`,
    "3#ג": `This tear in its length, etc. Tur wrote in this language: or a tear was made in it of the length of a tefach, or it was removed — treifah; and afterward he wrote: the flesh was bored in a circle or in length if the deficiency is only like a sela, etc. Beit Yosef in name of Mahari Chaviv — and this is his language: I did not know his intent, for if in length it is not boring and its measure is a tefach; but if in a circle, when you place a thread around the bore it will be a tefach in the thread — end of his words. Explanation: the sela is a third of a tefach, and whatever has a width of a tefach has a circumference of three; and in Kesef Mishneh he wrote on this; and to me it appears simple that when they said its measure is a tefach — it is in a tear and not deficient; and bored means it is deficient — therefore its measure is like a sela, end of his words. And he did not fix anything — for the essence of Mahari Chaviv's difficulty was from what Tur wrote first "if torn or removed," etc.; and removed means bored, and all the more according to Rambam as we will mention below. Rashba and Rashba answered Tur's words according to Rambam's intent that he copied here in Shulchan Aruch — namely that it was not torn or removed in mafpolash but in the thickness of the flesh; and afterward he wrote bored — meaning mafpolash. And Tur's language does not imply so. And it appears to me: first Tur came to teach that here it is not like the spleen where we find punctured in its support — treifah, and even if all of it was removed — kosher; therefore he wrote here: whether the majority was torn from it or all that kereis was removed — treifah, for there is no advantage to removal more than tear; and therefore he wrote "or removed" after writing "if torn" a tefach — to reveal that in removal he speaks of all of it. And one may say: why did he write this law — for it is not like the spleen specifically here and not in other limbs. One may say: because he gave a general rule in siman 50 that wherever they said "if punctured in any amount — treifah," the same if removed except the spleen — if so here too that its puncture is not in any amount, its law for removal would be like the spleen; he teaches that removal does not help here. And since he revealed that removal does not help here, he came to give it a measure afterward: if bored and flesh was removed from there, whether in a circle or in length — since there is deficiency, its measure is like a sela as above. And what Tur wrote "or removed in it" — the word "in it" is a scribal error and in some texts it is not; and more likely it was written "all of it," i.e., in this formulation, etc., and the copyist erred and wrote "in it" instead of "all of it." But in Shulchan Aruch's words here, which are Rambam's words, it does not reconcile with all we mentioned — for he wrote first "or removed," and removed means bored; and why in seif 4 did he write "or in length" — he already wrote its law. And it must be: he taught and then explained — first he wrote generally that there is treifah in tear and in removal; afterward he explains first the law of the measure of the tear until the end of the seif; and in seif 4 he explains the law of removal — meaning bored in a circle or in length.`,
    "4#א": `When they have no food. Tur wrote anonymously if they entered with difficulty; and Beit Yosef wondered why he did not mention the distinction between whether food is on it or not. Rashba wrote the reason: since we do not know how much the coating will be — therefore they were stringent that in every manner it is treifah even if they enter with difficulty without food.`,
    "4#ב": `That if this tear is stretched, etc. If bored in a circle it works well — for any circle whose width is a tefach has a circumference of three tefachim; but if bored in length it does not mean the surrounding thread will be a tefach and no more — rather this is what it says: if it is in length and when stretched from lip to lip until it is like a circle it will be a tefach — all that has a sela in length as above.`,
    "7#א": `In the place of their attachment. Rashi: the meses and beit hakosot — end of the kereis called paunch is shaped like a hat and called beit hakosot; the meses is attached to it; and around their attachment when you separate them there is a wall for this and a wall for that; and between them they pour one into the other; and food enters from beit hakosot to the meses and from the meses to the kivah and from the kivah to the dekin — therefore a puncture in the place of their attachment is not invalid, for in any case they pour into each other.`,
    "7#ב": `Mafpolash from side to side. To understand the dispute that Rama brings — we will copy their dispute: the rabbis taught: a needle found in the thickness of beit hakosot from one side — kosher; from two sides — treifah. Rashi: specifically in beit hakosot we require a mafpolash puncture; but in the meses even from one side where the puncture is not seen outside — this is not sealing, because the wall is very thin. Tosafot challenged him: we learned "the meses and beit hakosot punctured outward — treifah" — we learn that in the meses too we require mafpolash. R' Yitzchak says: this "outward" is not entirely outward but toward the outside — excluding punctured one into the other. And R' Yitzchak further says: we find to say that in the meses even from one side it is treifah, for we are concerned lest it punctured outward and returned and healed, as regarding esophagus siman 33. And that which we learned specifically punctured outward is treifah — when we know it did not heal, such as when it ate a needle before us and they slaughtered it immediately, end of his words. In name of Rabbenu Tam they wrote: in the meses too we require mafpolash like all treifot. And that which it specifies beit hakosot teaches that from one side it is kosher even though one whole skin was punctured and we are not concerned lest it healed — but regarding esophagus it is different, for it moves and contracts and sometimes the needle returns inside the esophagus; but in the meses if it healed it would not return inside. Tur and other poskim agreed with Rabbenu Tam, and so ruled here in Shulchan Aruch; and the "some forbid" of Rama is Rashi, and Terumat HaDeshen and Orach Chaim haArukh agreed; therefore Rama ruled we follow the lenient view in great loss.`,
    "7#ג": `In the meses even without a mafpolash puncture. It appears that since according to Rashi's reason — because there is no sealing here as I mentioned nearby in his name — this law applies even if we see the puncture was made by illness and the illness controlled only until the puncture came and no more — nevertheless treifah since there is no sealing. But according to what Tosafot say for Rashi's view — the reason "lest it punctured and healed" — there is a distinction: if the puncture was made by needle or thorn, then we are concerned lest it punctured entirely and healed, even if the needle is not before us; but if it is evident it was made by illness and the illness controlled only partially — kosher; and this is similar to what Tosafot say: if it ate a needle before us and they slaughtered it immediately and did not puncture outward — kosher. And it appears we rule thus; for also in esophagus we permit when we see the illness controlled only partially as written siman 33 — therefore even without great loss it is permitted in this.`,
    "8#א": `They turn it over and examine, etc. This is Tur's language — implying examination is obligatory even when punctured from one side alone; and Rama brought nearby "some are lenient" from one side, etc. — namely Rambam and Ra'ash as Beit Yosef wrote in their view, and so in Darkei Moshe. I say: first it appears proven from the Gemara that examination is required — there Rav Safra said to Abaye: see, master, this young scholar from the west said an incident came before Rabbi with a needle in beit hakosot from one side, and he said treifah; he sent to him and he did not come; Abaye went to him and said: tell me the body of the incident — where was it. He said: a needle came before Rabbi in the thickness of beit hakosot from one side; Rabbi turned it over and found korat dam on it and declared treifah, and said: if there is no wound outward — whence this blood. Abaye said: in vain did he trouble to go to him — for there is no novelty here; it is our mishnah "the meses and beit hakosot punctured outward." And if there were no obligation to examine when found from one side — why did he say there is no novelty; behold he informed us of a great novelty that there is obligation to turn over and examine as Rabbi did — for otherwise why did Rabbi turn it over. Rather it is clear to Abaye from the mishnah that punctured outward is treifah, and whatever emerged from one side — certainly we must see if there is korat dam, for then certainly the second side was also punctured, as in any safek treifot where we see a defect. And as they said in the mishnah: it fell to the ground and its innards were crushed — treifah; and it does not teach that examination is required; but in truth examination is required as stated siman 52 and in many places where there is a defect; and as we say in the first chapter of Chullin (daf 17): regarding examination of a knife — it is obvious that since it punctured — treifah requires examination. And this is certainly so — since a knife is commonly found to damage, it is as if a defect is before us. And it appears from this reason that Rambam also holds thus, as Beit Yosef proved from Rambam that he holds no examination is needed from one side — since he mentioned only treifah and did not mention examination from one side. And in what we said for Abaye — from the mishnah it was exactly so in Rambam: he need not mention examination since before us in puncture from two sides it is certainly treifah, and in puncture from one side it requires examination afterward. Ra'ash wrote: if he turned it over and examined, etc. — Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe learned from this language that examination is not obligatory. I wonder — for Tur also wrote this language in hints like Ra'ash, yet wrote here "they turn it over," etc., implying examination is obligatory. Rather it is simple: the language "if" refers to "if found on it blood" — and this is what it says: if when he turned it over he found blood on it, etc. An example: Ra'ash himself explained on "it is known" regarding "it fell to the ground," etc. — this is what it says: when it fell to the ground its innards were crushed, etc. And likewise here: examination is obligatory for everyone according to what I wrote; and so Rashba for halacha and Mahari Chaim z"l. And according to what Abaye learned from the mishnah that punctured from two sides is treifah — we learn that from one side one must examine since there is safek treifot before us. It is further clear: whatever was salted or the needle was lost and examination is impossible — treifah from safek; for we cannot say there is a distinction between one side and two sides — otherwise Abaye's difficulty returns: why did he say there is no novelty in Rabbi's incident — perhaps the novelty is that examination is not required from one side, just as it is lenient when examination is impossible. Rather it teaches there is obligation to examine. It is simply that there is no leniency when found from one side — neither regarding obligation of examination nor when examination is impossible — only that Rama wrote nearby one may be lenient from one side when examination is impossible; and so Rashba; and it appears to me as I wrote and as Shulchan Aruch ruled according to Rashba's responsum siman 360 that wherever examination is required — treifah if examination is impossible; as Rama himself brought nearby — therefore one should be stringent also here, not like Rama's leniency in great loss twice in this siman.`,
    "8#ב": `Around the needle. And all the more on it — Beit Yosef answered his difficulty: what difference between one side and two sides — in both, if there is blood — treifah, and without blood — kosher; and it works for him that from one side there is treifah only with blood opposite the needle; and from two sides treifah even if blood is around the needle. And it appears to me to distinguish: from one side there is no treifah when blood is found inside — for even though blood is before us proving it was from life, nevertheless only one skin was punctured — therefore specifically if blood is found outside it is treifah, for it is proven the second side was also punctured. But from two sides there is treifah even if blood is found inside — for here it is proven it was from life.`,
    "8#ג": `Found in the meses, etc. It appears that if there is korat dam outside — treifah, as Rama wrote siman 33 regarding esophagus; and so is implied from what is said here "and no examination is required" — implying in any case there is prohibition when korat dam is found outside; and the same for a wound. And see what I wrote siman 49 seif 3.`,
    "8#ד": `And one may rely on them if it is great loss and examination is impossible, etc. I wonder what he innovated here — for he already wrote this above. And it appears he did not write it here except to answer why there is leniency when the needle was lost or the flesh was salted and rinsed — for he wrote nearby at the beginning of this hagahah: wherever examination is required, etc., treifah; therefore he wrote that from one side examination itself is not obligatory according to some — therefore kosher when examination is impossible; and such are his words in Darkei Moshe. But it was already clarified above that examination is obligatory — therefore forbidden in every case where examination is impossible, for there is safek treifot. Hagahot Shaarei Dura siman 92 and Orach Chaim haArukh kelal 91 wrote: if a needle is found in the wall of beit hakosot along its length such that one cannot understand which side the pouch faces — treifah, since its cavity is wide; if through the esophagus it should have been found on the secretion — rather it certainly punctured and came, end of his words. Rashba brought this Orach Chaim siman 61 and wrote on this: "along its length" means the needle lies in the wall of beit hakosot along the length of the needle — and these are matters of taste; and one may not be lenient even from one side and from inside. Therefore there is no permit from one side except when the needle lies in the cavity of beit hakosot specifically as food goes — not stuck along the length of the wall. Also Darkei Moshe siman 46 brought this hagahah — I do not know why he did not bring it in Shulchan Aruch.`,
  },
  "siftei-kohen": {
    "1#_": `And if the chelev, etc. See above siman 64 seif 14.`,
    "10#_": `Treifah. As below siman 91.`,
    "11#א": `["<b>We suspend to be lenient.</b> Because the way of the esophagus is to swallow, and through there food enters — only the puncture was sealed; therefore one of the punctures was sealed — we suspend on what is its way; and further we established this bird on presumption of kashrut regarding these punctures — end of Rashba's words:"]`,
    "11#ב": `["<b>And it is like punctured from one side, etc.</b> Maharshal Orach Chaim siman 61 wrote: even according to Rashba — specifically in kereis, meses, and beit hakosot, where we establish on presumption of puncture wherever we did not examine (see s.k. 23 and 25); but kurkavan whose wall is thick — even for Rashi who wrote in the meses from one side treifah, they concede in kurkavan as Mahari wrote in Tur haArukh — if so, where not examined, even Rashba concedes we establish on presumption of kashrut — and this is clear until here his language. And this is not clear to me — for in Terumat HaDeshen there he wrote that even in beit hakosot Rashi concedes; but certainly one must say: for concern lest it healed we are not concerned, but for concern lest it punctured we are concerned; and so from Darkei Moshe's language that I brought in s.k. 40 — kurkavan is equal to beit hakosot in this:"]`,
    "11#ג": `["<b>And if there is no korat dam on it.</b> For if there is korat dam on it — even if you say it came through the face — it punctured outward; for if there is no wound there — whence korat dam:"]`,
    "11#ד": `["<b>Korat dam, etc.</b> He wrote in Darkei Moshe siman 49: examination from outside is required if there is no korat dam, according to those requiring examination when punctured from one side, end of his words; and it is implied where rinsed or salted its law is like punctured from one side:"]`,
    "11#ה": `["<b>And there is a distinction.</b> The reason is as Orach Chaim haArukh kelal 91 din 23 wrote: since the needle is not recognizable from inside as it is not recognizable from outside — one may suspend and say it came from outside and punctured the esophagus or the dekin, and so all similar cases, end of his words; and it is implied that wherever there is some recognition from inside, such as there is korat dam from inside and not from outside — kosher:"]`,
    "12#_": `And to forbid on his word. He said: whatever the needle is found has legs to speak — and it is like revealing a matter in the world, end of Rashba in responsum siman 204; and Maharam of Lublin wrote in responsum siman 66 on a gentile dealing with removing the innards from the animal and said "I found," etc., until "we need not believe him," end of his words — and see siman 127.`,
    "2#_": `Worms, etc. See above siman 36 s.k. 17 what he wrote from this.`,
    "3#א": `["<b>Flesh covering most of the kereis.</b> It is a thick membrane passing over the entire cavity; it extends from the chest to the thighs; and the kereis called paunch — its minority is hidden in the ribs of the chest and its majority is under that membrane. Tur and Rashi:"]`,
    "3#ב": `["<b>Most of the thickness of the flesh, etc.</b> And the view of Rashi, Rashba, Ran, and Rabbenu Yerucham is that it is treifah only when torn or removed in mafpolash until the inner kereis is visible; and so Maharam Alashkar in responsum siman 47, and so Maharshal Orach Chaim siman 60; and so it appears to me from Behag daf 129b, and Mordechai Orach Chaim in name of Ra'aviah — and this is the view of "some say" in Rif; and thereby what was difficult for Beit Yosef and Bach is resolved — examine well. And in Taz he brought two views on this:"]`,
    "3#ג": `["<b>Or removed.</b> From its place where there is deficiency:"]`,
    "3#ד": `["<b>Most of the length.</b> In Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe and other acharonim — the same applies to most of its width; and it is implied from their words that in a large animal whose measure is a tefach there is no distinction between its length and width — only because here the term "length" is used they had to write thus here specifically:"]`,
    "4#א": `["<b>Or in length.</b> Explanation: if it was bored — meaning deficient in mafpolash in length; and when you make a circle from the length it will be like a sela. And in seif 3 regarding removal — treifah in its length deals even where the strip is not wide, in a matter that if you make a circle from it there will not be a third of a tefach — nevertheless since its length is a tefach it is treifah as above — clear. Bach and Derishah wrote other forced explanations — see there. And especially what Darkei Moshe wrote: bored in length means when there is deficiency we estimate like in a circle — see there, implying even if the circumference of the strip in length is like the circumference of the circle. If so, why did they say in the Gemara "bored like a sela" — it should have said "bored needs a tefach in its circumference" as said regarding tear — examine well. And see above siman 30 s.k. 6:"]`,
    "4#ב": `["<b>And it is so that three can enter, etc.</b> Rambam's and Tur's language — so that three date pits enter one next to the other with difficulty; and so Maharal there:"]`,
    "5#_": `If it dissolved, etc. See above siman 43 seif 2 and end of siman 50.`,
    "7#א": `["<b>Kosher.</b> For in any case they pour into each other. Rashi and poskim:"]`,
    "7#ב": `["<b>And if the puncture is on other sides.</b> Tosafot wrote, and Bach brings them: even if both skins were punctured in the place where it lies on the meses and is stuck there in fat — it does not seal, for it is tame chelev, as below siman 64 seif 8; and so is implied in Tur Choshen Mishpat beginning kelal 95:"]`,
    "7#ג": `["<b>And some forbid, etc.</b> In Darkei Moshe and Tur Choshen Mishpat there he concludes; however in Tur haArukh siman 165 he ruled like Rashi; and so Orach Chaim haArukh — we practice like Rashi. Rashi's reason: since it has no thick skin but thin skin — we are concerned lest it punctured from wall to wall and healed; unlike in the thickness of beit hakosot. And it is implied he forbids only when the puncture came through a needle — from concern lest it punctured and healed; and so Tur Choshen Mishpat and Orach Chaim haArukh kelal 91 — some forbid from this reason; and so he wrote in his signs in Tur Choshen Mishpat there — and so is the practice unless in great loss or time of pressure, since its wall is thin — we are concerned lest it punctured and healed, end of his words. But when it is evident it came through illness — there is no such concern — kosher; and so in poskim in name of Rashi; and so in Sefer Lechem siman 162b — it appears also Rashi forbids only through a needle, for in all places Rashi wrote this it refers only to a needle, end of his words. And so from Rashi's words in the mishnah "the meses and beit hakosot punctured outward" — Derishah Orach Chaim explains: punctured outward means the puncture is seen from outside, such as when this or that was punctured not in the place of their attachment, etc., end of his words. And so Hagahot Shaarei Dura siman 94 in name of R' Yehudah Zahav — Rashi deals here with a puncture not through a needle, meaning why is it not treifah until the puncture is seen from outside; but through a needle it is treifah in the meses from one side or in the thin place of beit hakosot for the reason mentioned. Maharshal in his Orach Chaim there did not understand R' Yehudah Zahav's words and strained greatly — the matter is simple as I wrote, and not as Bach wrote that Rashi forbids also through illness and ruled so even not in great loss — which is not clear at all; and even if Rashi's view were so, all poskim wrote not like him, and all acharonim forbade only through a needle; and so Taz and Lechem there:"]`,
    "7#ד": `["<b>In the meses, etc.</b> Derishah wrote: even though Rashi mentions beit hakosot — around its lip there is thickness, etc. — one should not interpret that in the rest of beit hakosot the law is like the meses; rather it appears to me all of beit hakosot is doubled and thick, etc., end of his words. Bach in his last pamphlet challenged his words as not appearing at all — and there is no doubt they omitted Rabbenu Yerucham's words netiv 10 ot 10, who wrote explicitly that for Rashi in beit hakosot in the thin place it is treifah; and so Orach Chaim haArukh kelal 91 din 11; and so from Ran Orach Chaim — and this is his language: beit hakosot — part of it is thick and has two skins, and part thin with only one skin; and he says a needle found in the thick place — if from one side, meaning only one skin was punctured — kosher, end of his words. (And what Derishah wrote "it appears all of beit hakosot is doubled and thick," etc. — the known fact and poskim refute this.) And so from Semak siman 141, brought in Hagahot Shaarei Dura — and this is his language: it implies that if the needle is found in the kereis not in the thick place of beit hakosot — even from one side treifah, etc. And what Derishah wrote "let it distinguish and teach in the matter itself," etc. — it is no difficulty at all — see there. And see s.k. 22:"]`,
    "7#ה": `["<b>Even without mafpolash puncture, etc.</b> The reason is already explained — we are concerned lest it punctured outward and the needle returned backward and healed; and it is implied according to this view that even examination to see if it punctured outward does not help — lest it healed so much it is not recognizable; and so Ran, and so Rashba in Tur haArukh long daf 38b, and so Tosafot Orach Chaim and chapter "With what may we light" and other places that reconcile: that which we learned punctured outward — we are not concerned lest it healed with slight changes; and if so it should have taught that we examined the other side — examine well. And it is implied in the meses treifah even if there is no korat dam outside; and so Taz — and it is simple that if there is korat dam outside, even beit hakosot is treifah as nearby seif 8. However it appears we require korat dam from inside — otherwise we would say after slaughter it stuck in the meses and before slaughter it did not stick at all; for if it stuck before slaughter, blood should have flowed; and so Tur Choshen Mishpat beginning kelal 94 — see there and in his signs:"]`,
    "7#ו": `["<b>If not in a place of great loss, etc.</b> Bach forbade through a needle even in great loss — which is not clear at all; and so is Lechem's view like Rabbenu Yerucham:"]`,
    "8#א": `["<b>In one of them.</b> Meaning in one of those mentioned — in the meses or in beit hakosot — and it is obvious:"]`,
    "8#ב": `["<b>If found on it, etc.</b> But if korat dam is found around the puncture — kosher until korat dam is found opposite the mouth of the needle. Mordechai in name of Ra'aviah and other great ones, and Beit Yosef and Tur Choshen Mishpat:"]`,
    "8#ג": `["<b>Korat dam, etc.</b> For if there is no wound there — whence korat dam. Gemara:"]`,
    "8#ד": `["<b>And if not — kosher.</b> It implies even in the meses according to the Mechaber and his supporters; and to resolve "a thorn stuck in esophagus" above siman 33 — we are concerned lest it healed — one may say: certainly the kereis is at rest forever; if it had passed from side to side initially as then, it should be found now; and crust could not rise there because the standing needle prevented it from rising; and since now only one side is found — it was thus initially and never punctured from side to side. But regarding esophagus that ate into it and moved and contracted and stretched it — as the Gemara says in the first chapter of Orach Chaim — since it always moves, there is concern lest initially both skins of the esophagus were punctured from side to side and through movement the needle returned somewhat inside and then crust rose from outside; for presumably crust cannot rise while the needle is there — end of Ra'af in Semak siman 141 daf 96, brought in Hagahot Shaarei Dura; and so Ra'ash concludes Orach Chaim in name of Rabbenu Tam:"]`,
    "8#ה": `["<b>Kosher.</b> Already explained that there are disputants in the meses and we practice to declare treifah not in great loss — and see s.k. 14:"]`,
    "8#ו": `["<b>Even if only a bit remained, etc.</b> So ruled Terumat HaDeshen siman 165 — see there; and he proved so with clear proofs — and not like Bach:"]`,
    "8#ז": `["<b>Only a bit of outer skin.</b> Of beit hakosot — even for those who forbid in the meses, here they concede as he proved in Tur haArukh there; and he wrote the reason: in something whose wall is thin there is concern lest it healed because a needle's way is not to puncture and enter only a bit along its length; and in a thin wall a small amount that clings will pass the wall. But in a thick wall where much can enter and still not pass the entire wall — we cannot say it healed — and see there where he elaborated with proofs. All this is not like Sefer Torat Chaim daf 134 — he wrote here things that do not appear; and he omitted Terumat HaDeshen's words mentioned — see there:"]`,
    "8#ח": `["<b>And this is when they did not salt, etc.</b> The Mechaber learned this from Rashba's responsum; and so acharonim — see Rashba. And it appears to me Rashba also concedes where they salted or rinsed it is kosher; and specifically where the needle was removed and because of its thinness the place of puncture is not recognizable and examination is impossible whether it punctured outward — Rashba wrote in responsum siman 243 treifah because examination is required to permit it, for it stands in safek prohibition lest it punctured outward; therefore as long as they did not examine to remove it from presumption of prohibition — treifah. But when it is known it did not puncture outward — then it is in presumption of permissibility; and examination to see if there is korat dam there is to remove it from presumption of permissibility — if so, as long as there is clearly no korat dam — kosher:"]`,
    "8#ט": `["<b>And even the point of the thorn, etc.</b> Meaning its point is toward the cavity of beit hakosot and the thick head is toward outside — such a case regarding liver siman 41 is treifah, for we say the way of the esophagus entered the dekin and punctured the dekin; here since there are foods and liquids we say foods and liquids pressed it. Gemara; and Tur Choshen Mishpat beginning kelal 94; and there is no distinction whether the needle is small or large:"]`,
    "8#י": `["<b>Or it was lost.</b> It appears to interpret: the beit hakosot was lost and it was known it did not puncture outward, as written at the beginning if it did not puncture from side to side; but where it was lost and it was not known whether it punctured outward — treifah even in great loss. But Tur Choshen Mishpat kelal 94 din 2 and his signs there imply he permits even in such a case in great loss. And it requires further study — for it is implied from what he brought in name of Rashba's responsum siman 360 that if they took the needle from there without examination it is as if rinsed or salted, end of his words. If so, just as in rinse or salt we permit in great loss, so here; and in Rashba's responsum nothing is found; and so from Bach — in such a case treifah even in great loss. However Maharshal's view there appears to permit even in such a case; and so Ra'ash; and as I wrote in s.k. 32:"]`,
    "8#כ": `["<b>Around the needle.</b> He wrote in hagahot at end of Orach Chaim haArukh: presumably if it is somewhat far from the puncture like the width of a straw — likewise treifah, etc.; Bach brings this; and Maharshal and Bach wrote: punctured from two sides — treifah when korat dam is found around the needle whether inside or outside; and so Derishah; and so in Perishah end of siman 37:"]`,
    "8#ל": `["<b>And if not — after slaughter, etc.</b> For since the needle is here to draw blood — if it had punctured before slaughter there would have been flowing blood. Gemara:"]`,
    "8#מ": `["<b>If they did not salt, etc.</b> In this all agree: since it punctured from two sides it is in presumption of prohibition; and examination is to permit it to remove it from presumption of prohibition; and as long as it is not clearly known there is no korat dam — it remains in its prohibition:"]`,
    "8#נ": `["<b>Every place, etc.</b> Question: for what need did the rabbi write this — from where does it follow; one may say lost or they took the needle is preferable to rinsed or salted; and further he already wrote above in the hagahah "or lost," etc., implying lost is like salted and rinsed. And it appears he came to inform us that lost and taking the needle are equal in all its laws to salting and rinsing; and there is a difference where punctured from one side and before examining whether it punctured outward it was lost or they took the needle — kosher in great loss like in salt and rinse; and so I wrote in s.k. 25 — this is Rabbenu Yerucham's view in Tur Choshen Mishpat:"]`,
    "8#ס": `["<b>Since it punctured from both sides, etc.</b> And it appears the same in the meses: if it did not puncture from both sides — kosher when there is no korat dam at all, even for those who forbid in the meses — when there is korat dam inside, as in s.k. 14; and that itself is a general stringency. And do not add that we are not expert — and further those who forbid forbid from concern lest it healed — if so there are many doubts here. And further regarding puncture from both sides Bach wrote one may permit in great loss in our time; and so one should rule — for Maharshal and other acharonim did not write this stringency of "we are not expert" at all; and Taz omitted it:"]`,
    "8#ע": `["<b>And no examination is required.</b> It implies: all that is found is korat dam from outside — even if not stuck at all — treifah; for if there is no wound — whence korat dam; and so Rabbenu Yerucham's view in the hagahah above siman 33 seif 9 regarding esophagus — and see there:"]`,
    "8#פ": `["<b>And some are lenient, etc.</b> So is written in Darkei Moshe and Tur Choshen Mishpat — see Rambam and Ra'ash; and he followed Beit Yosef who wrote so is implied from Rambam's words and from Ra'ash: if he turned it over and found korat dam — treifah, end of his words. Bach wrote on this — and this is his language: I wonder, etc., until "and not like Beit Yosef and Rabbenu Yerucham in Shulchan Aruch who thought about Rambam and Ra'ash a law that is not true," end of his language. From his words it is implied his intent is to challenge that examination is required after korat dam; for if only to see whether it punctured outward — Beit Yosef and Rabbenu Yerucham did not mention this at all. If so, what proof does he bring from "seeing" there — one must see whether it punctured outward, and the same here since it is stuck before us — examination is required if it did not puncture on the other side as on this side. But after korat dam — whence that examination is required; perhaps just as there is no korat dam inside, there is none outside. And further wonder: how did he mix Ra'ash's language and challenge Beit Yosef and the rabbi — he omitted Ra'ash's words regarding "a thorn stuck in esophagus" who ruled no examination is required if there is a puncture, and brought proof from beit hakosot; and Bach himself siman 33 seif 8 for Ra'ash's view — see there. Thus in beit hakosot it is obvious no examination is required (and Beit Yosef and Rabbenu Yerucham also omitted these words of Ra'ash — examine well). And one may wonder on Tur who wrote here "they turn over and examine," etc. — and from what he wrote above siman 33 and Orach Chaim in hints it is clear Ra'ash requires no examination for esophagus — all the more here. And even though for lung we permit only with blowing, as Ra'ash and Tur themselves wrote end of siman 36 — see Beit Yosef — one must distinguish: there as long as they do not blow it there is concern lest there is a puncture before us not recognizable; not so here, since according to estimation it is not punctured outward — no need to turn over and examine. And one may say lung is different because its membranes are very thin; and so Maharam distinguished in his book daf 162a; and one may distinguish in several manners — examine well:"]`,
    "8#צ": `["<b>And one may rely on them, etc.</b> Bach wrote: it is implied for those requiring examination from one side one may not permit if salted or rinsed; and above he wrote in the hagahah that although ideally one must examine, nevertheless b'dieved where rinsed and salted — kosher; and here he retracted his words, end of his words. I see no contradiction or retraction here: above he brought Semak's view as Tur Choshen Mishpat implies from his words that from one side he permits b'dieved; and Semak did not mention that no examination is required ideally. Here he brought Ra'ash and Rambam's view that they do not require examination at all — to teach that on them one may rely even in our time in great loss. Now the general rule here "some are lenient" and "one may rely on them" — he already wrote above; rather he came to inform us: nevertheless in our time since we are not expert — if so, since there is a defect of salting and rinsing, one should declare treifah lest korat dam is before us and we are not expert; we rely on lenient views only from one side; and so is clear in Darkei Moshe and Tur Choshen Mishpat who wrote where punctured from one side one may permit even in our time since this is not great expertise, etc. And further Rambam and Ra'ash hold no examination is required even ideally, end of his words — and this is clear:"]`,
    "8#ק": `["<b>And examination is impossible, etc.</b> But where he examined and there is no korat dam outside — kosher even not in great loss; and specifically in beit hakosot. But in the meses, even if there is no korat dam outside but only inside — treifah not in great loss, as explained in s.k. 14:"]`,
    "9#_": `And likewise if, etc. Maharshal there wrote: regarding puncture from one side, rust neither raises nor lowers; and he also wrote in name of Maharam Mintz: rust in the middle of the needle — kosher; and Bach declares treifah in every case.`,
  },
  "baer-heitev": {
    "1#_": `On the yeter. Taz wrote: even though we rule in siman 64 that chelev on the yeter is forbidden and tame chelev does not seal — nevertheless since the people of Eretz Yisrael eat chelev on the yeter, it helps the people of Bavel to permit sealing the puncture (but chelev on the arch is forbidden to everyone — therefore it does not seal. It turns out: a peg or needle stuck at the end of the animal's kivah and the kivah was not punctured from side to side, only a hard growth called wart stands on the kivah opposite the place the needle is stuck from inside — he answered in Shulchan Aruch siman 62 that the wart is proof it was punctured and it is crust that rose from a wound, except one may permit because the wart was covered with chelev on the yeter; and although black stain was found in the chelev where it adhered to the wart — nevertheless there was so much thick chelev outside and they saw no black stain outside — therefore it is not korat dam from outside and did not puncture from side to side, end of his words. And so Peri Chadash — even if peg or needle is found stuck in the wall of the kivah, if a bit of skin remains that did not puncture from side to side — one may permit for everyone. And see Tzitz Eliezer siman 49 who leans to be stringent. In Tur haArukh siman 5 he ruled to declare treifah a kivah from which emerged three branches a half-finger in length and more like pieces of intestine and it was closed at the end, but the flesh was like kivah flesh without any change; however he permitted and declared kosher based on testimony that this is very common in many fat animals because of the fat — see there):`,
    "10#_": `Treifah. Orach Chaim haArukh wrote: if a needle is found in the wall of beit hakosot along its length such that one cannot understand which side the pouch faces — treifah, since its cavity is wide; if through the esophagus it should have been found on the secretion — rather it certainly punctured and came. Maharshal explained: along its length means the needle lies in the wall of beit hakosot along the length of the needle — and one may not be lenient even from one side and from inside. Therefore there is no permit from one side except when the needle lies in the cavity of beit hakosot as food goes — not when stuck along the length of the wall.`,
    "11#א": `To be lenient. The reason is in Shach in name of Rashba: the way of the esophagus is to swallow, and through there food enters with the eaters — only the puncture was sealed; therefore one of the punctures was sealed — we suspend on what is its way; and further we established it on presumption of kashrut regarding these punctures.`,
    "11#ב": `Like punctured. Maharshal wrote: regarding kurkavan all agree that if not examined we establish on presumption of kashrut. Shach disagrees — for although we are not concerned lest it healed, we are concerned lest it punctured.`,
    "11#ג": `Kosher. Shach wrote: but if there is korat dam there — even if you say it came through the face — it punctured outward; for if there is no wound — whence korat dam. He wrote in Darkei Moshe: examination from outside is required according to those requiring examination from one side; and Shach wrote it is implied where rinsed or salted its law is like punctured from one side. Taz wrote: if examination is impossible, such as lost after the needle was found — one forbids from safek since examination is required and we do not say shechutah hutrah; and so Maharil in an incident where a child cried while eating that there is a needle in the kurkavan and forbade everything even mixtures. Shach wrote: the reason those forbid even without korat dam outside is because the needle is not recognizable from inside as from outside — we fear the needle came from outside and punctured other organs. It is implied from Orach Chaim haArukh: whatever recognition is from inside and not outside — such as korat dam inside and not outside — kosher.`,
    "12#_": `Sharp. The reason Rashba wrote: whatever the needle is found has legs to speak — and it is like revealing a matter in the world (and if a gentile said he found — see end of siman 127 — it is a simple matter a gentile is not believed at all in this; a minor is preferable to a gentile; see Taz who brought an incident with a gentile dealing with removing innards until "we need not believe him" — see there; and see Peri Chadash):`,
    "2#_": `The chelev. Taz challenged: we find proper sealing — if punctured where the spleen is attached. And one must say: since the membrane on the spleen is forbidden as full chelev as written siman 64 seif 10 — therefore there is no sealing for the kereis, for tame chelev does not seal (a gentile who found a peg in the kereis — if he is believed to him, forbidden; even though the gentile says it was not stuck but only partly in the wall of the kereis inside — the gentile may not know to aim; but if he says he found inside the folds of the meses called heib — one does not forbid. Taz end of siman 50. A chicken whose stomach was swollen — after slaughter when opened, clear liquid squirted from it — kosher; responsum of Bach siman 157; but in an animal such a case Peri Chadash siman 50 s.k. 1 wrote one may not permit except in great loss. And in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah siman 61 he heard from doctors there is danger from eating this, lest one also come to an illness called Wassersucht. A puncture found in the wall opposite the kereis — we are not concerned lest the kereis was punctured — kosher; see there siman 130. And what the Mechaber wrote "for the chelev on it is forbidden" — this is for those who forbid chelev adhering to the kereis under the diaphragm; but the people of Rhineland who practice leniency to eat it as written siman 64 in the hagahah — all the more if the kereis was punctured this chelev seals it (Peri Chadash). And what Rama permitted regarding worms in the kereis — Maharam Tikotin in name of Taz wrote: specifically when found placed inside; but if they go outside and it is not known if they left before slaughter — it appears their law is like a healthy animal in siman 36 — see there seif 5, end of his words:`,
    "3#א": `Thickness. Shach brings the view of Rashi, Rashba, and several great poskim that they permit as long as it was not punctured mafpolash; and Taz wrote: nevertheless one may not be lenient since they established in Shulchan Aruch for halacha; in any case it is clear that also regarding mafpolash he forbids only at a tefach. And all this when punctured through illness; but through needle or thorn — treifah in every manner as siman 91 — see there (lest the intestines were punctured) (Taz). An animal gored by its fellow in the horn in the place of the outer kereis and it is evident the place became raised at the goring until it appears the flesh covering the outer kereis was torn and the skin is whole — if it is known the covering flesh was torn at the measure for which it becomes treifah, namely length of one tefach — it is treifah from life and its milk is forbidden as milk of a treifah; but if it is not known whether the puncture is a tefach long — it appears one may not forbid the milk from life from safek. But if it was slaughtered and the tear of a tefach measure was not found — nevertheless one must check the inner organs punctured in any amount (Beit Yosef siman 13; and see Kenesset HaGedolah 168). (Tzitz Eliezer siman 49: if a needle is found stuck in the kereis and opposite that place a adhesion is found in the kereis — we suspend stringently, for certainly the adhesion came from the needle that punctured the kereis from side to side. But if a thorn or needle is placed thus in the kereis — even though beit hakosot adhered to some place — one does not declare treifah, end of his words):`,
    "3#ב": `Length. Shach wrote: the same applies to most of the width; and also in a large animal whose measure is a tefach there is no distinction between length and width.`,
    "4#א": `Like a sela. Shach explained: meaning deficient in mafpolash in length; and when you make a circle from the length it will be like a sela. And in seif 3 regarding removal — treifah in its length deals even where the strip is not wide, in a matter that if you make a circle there will not be a third of a tefach — nevertheless since its length is a tefach it is treifah. And Taz wrote: the Mechaber taught and then explained — first he wrote generally that there is treifah in tear and removal; afterward he explains first the law of the measure of the tear until the end of the seif; and in seif 4 he explains the law of removal — meaning bored in a circle or in length.`,
    "4#ב": `Loosely. Tur wrote anonymously if they entered with difficulty and did not distinguish between whether food is on it or not; and Rashba wrote the reason: since we do not know how much the coating will be — therefore they were stringent that in every manner it is treifah even if they enter with difficulty without food. And Taz wrote: one should wonder — if bored in a circle it works well that whatever has width of a tefach has circumference of three tefachim; but if bored in length it does not mean the surrounding thread will be a tefach — rather: if it is in length and stretches from lip to lip until it is like a circle, it will be a tefach — all that has a sela in length.`,
    "5#_": `Dissolved. See above siman 43 seif 2 and end of siman 50.`,
    "7#א": `Mafpolash. For in any case they pour into each other; and some say the puncture goes from this cavity to that cavity — but even if only the meses alone or beit hakosot alone was punctured in the place of their attachment — kosher, because the meses wall protects beit hakosot punctures and beit hakosot wall protects meses punctures.`,
    "7#ב": `Treifah. Shach wrote: even if both skins were punctured in the place where it lies on the meses and is stuck there in fat — it does not seal, for it is tame chelev. And what is called meses and beit hakosot — Rashi: end of the kereis called paunch shaped like a hat called beit hakosot; meses attached to it; around their attachment when you separate them there is a wall for each; between them they pour into each other; food enters from beit hakosot to meses and from meses to kivah and from kivah to dekin.`,
    "7#ג": `Practice. The reason is in Shach: since its wall is thin — we are concerned lest it punctured and healed — and all this specifically when punctured through thorn or needle (and even examination does not help to see if punctured outward); but when evident it came through illness — kosher even without great loss. And not specifically in the meses — the same in beit hakosot in the thin place. And he wrote further: it is implied in the meses treifah even if there is no korat dam outside; however it appears we require korat dam from inside — otherwise we would say after slaughter it stuck in the meses, and if it stuck before slaughter blood should have flowed (and know: beit hakosot — part thick with two skins, part thin with only one skin; and according to Rashi and those who forbid — in the thin place too treifah like the meses. So too R' Yehudah Zahav).`,
    "7#ד": `Those who permit. Shach wrote: not like Bach who forbids through a needle even in great loss (and Peri Chadash wrote we permit entirely — see there):`,
    "8#א": `And they examine. Taz wrote: this examination is obligatory even when punctured from one side — not like Rama who is lenient in great loss.`,
    "8#ב": `On it. Shach wrote: but if korat dam is found around the puncture — kosher until korat dam is found opposite the mouth of the needle.`,
    "8#ג": `Kosher. Shach wrote: it implies even in the meses; and it is not comparable to "a thorn stuck in esophagus" above siman 33 where we are concerned lest it healed — one may say because the kereis is at rest forever; if it had passed from side to side as then it should be found now; and crust could not rise there. But regarding esophagus that always moves, as the Gemara says — there is concern lest initially both skins were punctured from side to side and through movement the needle returned inside and crust rose from outside; and so according to the Mechaber; but we already explained above in the hagahah that we practice to declare treifah in the meses if not in great loss.`,
    "8#ד": `Punctured. Shach wrote: even for those who forbid in the meses — they concede in beit hakosot it is kosher since its wall is thick — we are not concerned lest it healed.`,
    "8#ה": `Outside. Even though regarding liver siman 41 it is treifah — here since there are foods and liquids we say foods and liquids pressed it and the dekin were not punctured; and there is no distinction whether the needle is small or large.`,
    "8#ו": `Lost. Shach wrote: it appears to interpret when beit hakosot was lost and it was known it did not puncture outward; but where lost and not known whether it punctured outward — treifah even in great loss; and so Taz. However other poskim permit even in this (and all the more if certainly known it did not puncture outward, only whether there is korat dam outside was unknown and it was lost — see in Shach. And Peri Chadash ruled to be lenient specifically if known it did not puncture outward; but where lost and not known whether punctured outward — for everyone treifah — see there; requires further study):`,
    "8#ז": `Around. (And this is the distinction between one side and two sides: from one side specifically on it; and from two sides even around. And another distinction: from one side if korat dam is found inside — kosher until korat dam is found outside; and from two sides even if found inside — treifah; and so acharonim — from this it is proven it was from life.) Shach wrote: even if somewhat far from the puncture like the width of a straw — likewise treifah; and if punctured from two sides and korat dam is found whether inside or outside — treifah; and so Taz.`,
    "8#ח": `They rinsed. Shach wrote: in this all agree — since it punctured from two sides it is in presumption of prohibition; examination is to permit it; and as long as we do not clearly know there is no korat dam — it remains in presumption of prohibition.`,
    "8#ט": `Lost. Shach wrote: Rama comes to inform us when lost and they took the needle — its law is equal in all respects to salting and rinsing; and the practical difference is where punctured from one side and before examination it was lost or they took the needle from there — kosher in great loss like in salt and rinse:`,
    "8#י": `Our examination. Shach wrote in name of Bach and Maharshal: in great loss one may permit even in our time. And if it punctured only from one side — one may permit in the meses even according to Rama if there is no korat dam — on this we rely on our examination even in our time.`,
    "8#כ": `Examination. Taz and Shach wrote: but if korat dam is found outside — even if not stuck at all — treifah; and see siman 33 seif 9 (for if there is no wound there — whence korat dam; and the same for a wound).`,
    "8#ל": `Great loss. Shach wrote: but where he examined and there is no korat dam outside — kosher even not in great loss; and specifically in beit hakosot. But in the meses, even if no korat dam outside but only inside — treifah not in great loss. And do not challenge: regarding lung, according to everyone we permit only with examination — one must say lung is different because its membranes are very thin. Taz disagrees with Rama and rules one may not rely on the lenient views even in great loss — see there (Taz in name of Shaarei Dura wrote: there is no permit from one side except when the needle lies in the cavity of beit hakosot as food goes — not stuck along the length of the wall; if lying in the wall of beit hakosot along its length — even from one side and from inside forbidden, since its cavity is wide; if through the esophagus it should have been found on the secretion — rather it certainly punctured and came. And see Shach, Taz, and Peri Chadash. Needle found in thickness of kereis and did not emerge outside — some students wanted to declare treifah; Radbaz siman 113 agreed with the butchers who permitted it. Beit hakosot — two skins punctured not opposite each other — kosher, for they are at rest. Gemara; Shach):`,
    "9#_": `Rust. Maharshal disagrees and holds regarding puncture from one side rust neither raises nor lowers; and he also wrote in name of Maharam Mintz: rust in the middle of the needle — kosher; and Bach declares treifah in every case (and so Peri Chadash and Kenesset HaGedolah; and if the needle was lost before examination whether rust was on it — kosher when punctured from one side; see Kenesset HaGedolah daf 141).`,
  },
  "beer-hagolah": {
    "1#_": `Mishnah Chullin daf 42.`,
    "10#_": `Beit Yosef according to his view, and so Rashba in his responsum.`,
    "11#_": `Mishnah there daf 42, and as Rashi explains there.`,
    "12#_": `Tosafot in name of Rabbenu Tam there in Shabbat daf 36; and so agreed Rashba, Ran, and Mordechai in name of Ra'aviah in name of his father, and Tur.`,
    "2#_": `There daf 49 and 50, and the first version stringently — Rif; and so is implied from Rambam's words in chapter 6. (°) Explanation: the kivah is shaped like an arch; outside is called the arch and inside the circle is called yeter.`,
    "3#_": `There in the mishnah.`,
    "4#_": `Rambam's language in chapter 6 of Hilchot Shechitah law 11.`,
    "5#_": `Rambam's language in chapter 9 of Hilchot Shechitah (law 5 and 6) from the mishnah there daf 42, and as R' Yosei bar Chanina explains stringently daf 52; and so Rashba in Tur haArukh and Ra'ash and Semak and Mordechai.`,
    "6#_": `Like R' Yehudah in the mishnah there, and as R' Binyamin bar Yefet explains there, etc. — Rif and Rambam there and Semak.`,
    "7#_": `And Beit Yosef wrote: and it appears to me that the same applies to most of its width, for we taught anonymously.`,
    "8#_": `Rambam's language there — the statement of Rav Geviha, etc., and of Rav Yosef there.`,
    "9#_": `The statement of Rav Yehudah, etc., there daf 43, and as Rabbenu Efrayim, Ra'ash, and Rashba in Tur haArukh.`,
  },
  "beur-hagra": {
    "1#_": `And if, etc. Chullin 49b — Rava said, etc.; and Rashi 50a s.v. kaf, etc.; and Tosafot there s.v. daiytra, etc.`,
    "10#_": `That which we said, etc. Tosafot s.v. machat; and as written there 53b regarding thorn, etc.`,
    "11#א": `Needle found, etc.; "for the needle," etc. Like a needle found in lung — and the large bronchus of the lung; and we say "bronchus" was mentioned; and all the more in such a case we say the way of the esophagus with the eaters entered — it is closer than those; and since the way of the esophagus is to swallow — one of the punctures was sealed — we suspend on what is its way.`,
    "11#ב": `And if there is no, etc. Even if punctured from the inside, if there is korat dam outside — treifah, as in the Gemara and as I wrote seif 8; and see siman 49 seif 3; and it means ideally examination is required according to the above Rashba. Darkei Moshe.`,
    "12#_": `Minor, etc. Whatever the needle is found has legs to speak — and a minor is believed like what is written in the second chapter of Ketuvot. There.`,
    "2#א": `Kereis, etc. Like R' Yosei bar Chanina there 50b.`,
    "2#ב": `And it has nothing, etc. See above siman 64 seif 9.`,
    "2#ג": `Worms, etc. End of chapter 3 seif 7.`,
    "3#א": `And it, etc., "rather since," etc. All this is Rambam's language; he explains what is written in the Gemara 52b "flesh covering most of the kereis in its majority" — meaning in the majority of its thickness; and all poskim disagree with him and wrote specifically mafpolash as the plain meaning of the sugya. (Collection:) Even though not, etc. — he learned from what is written "torn" and they did not say "pierced" as in gullet and spinal cord; and as written in the first chapter of Gittin — they tear paper for them, etc.; but in Tur haArukh he disagreed and said: why "torn" — because it is severed, specifically in all of it or its majority, unlike where even a tefach in a large animal, etc. — see there at length. And according to Tosafot in Gittin — there too they tear from side to side — see there 9b s.v. mikra'in, etc. (end of collection).`,
    "3#ב": `Since, etc., "or," etc. There Rav Ashi asked regarding majority, etc.; and Rashi there s.v. b'rov, etc.; and therefore it must be so — since it says "extend," etc., and he ruled stringently; and further we rule like R' Yosei bar Chanina and the question was resolved.`,
    "3#ג": `And how much, etc. Like R' Yehudah — all amoraim there hold like him.`,
    "4#_": `(Collection) If it was, etc. Like Rabbah and Rav Yosef; but according to Tosafot there 54b s.v. k'sela we rule like Rav Geviha. Tur haArukh 43a (end of collection).`,
    "7#א": `And if they were punctured, etc. Mishnah 54a.`,
    "7#ב": `And if the puncture, etc. Mishnah 42a.`,
    "7#ג": `And some, etc. From what is stated 50b: the rabbis taught regarding a needle, etc.; and since it left the meses' mate — we learn that there too from one side it is treifah; and as stated in the second chapter of Shabbat for the conclusion regarding a needle, etc.; and so they said in chapter 3 of Sukkah; and that which the mishnah says punctured outward — Rashi explained: excluding punctured one into the other, as in the mishnah below, as above; and Tosafot there and there and there were precise about "outward"; and further that there is no treifah when not mafpolash — see there. (Collection) And some forbid, etc. — his words are not correct; Rashi does not disagree except regarding a needle, as stated second chapter of Shabbat and chapter 3 of Sukkah — we are concerned as in esophagus siman 33 seif 9; but without a needle it is not worse than esophagus, as written siman 64. (And even though in Tosafot and Ra'ash it appears the same without a needle — nevertheless one may say their words as above.) And it appears Rashi's words are primary as the plain meaning of the Gemara; and how forced is Rabbenu Tam's explanation in that Gemara; and likewise in the beraisa of Chullin 50b only beit hakosot is mentioned; and so there 49a; and so Ra'ash there 64 to rule like Rav Alfas that we rule like him; and it is possible, etc.; and even though one may say as Rabbenu Tam because it moves, etc.; and so in Hagahot Maimoniyot there s.v. aval, etc. — two reasons to distinguish — see there. Nevertheless the primary reason is the first, as above. And afterward I found in Tosafot Shabbat 36a s.v. l'machat in name of R' Yitzchak likewise — see there what he wrote "and that which it asks," etc. One may also say as Ra'ash in the first chapter of Chullin — such as punctured through illness — and that is the truth (end of collection).`,
    "8#א": `Or a thorn. Tosafot s.v. ki'van, etc. — see there.`,
    "8#ב": `Even if not, etc. As stated in the mishnah "the meses and beit hakosot punctured outward"; and according to Rabbenu Tam even in the meses the entire skin must be punctured; and even according to Rashi we are concerned lest it healed in the meses specifically because its wall is thin — and this is what the beraisa means "from two sides treifah" — meaning visible from both sides.`,
    "8#ג": `And this is when they did not salt, etc. As stated in Avodah Zarah 32b; and according to Rashba's view even from one side it is not kosher without examination.`,
    "8#ד": `And some are lenient, etc. As in Hagahot Semak that Tur brought; and see Darkei Moshe; and see "some are lenient," etc., and "one may rely," etc., and as written here.`,
    "8#ה": `And it is still, etc. As written below "for since," etc., "but," etc.`,
    "8#ו": `Around, etc. As above — even from one side examination is required; and that which the beraisa teaches "korat dam found on it," etc. — applies whether from one side or two sides; and what is taught "from one side kosher," etc. — when found around the needle; and see Rashi s.v. u'matzah, etc.`,
    "8#ז": `If they did not salt, etc. — and here according to Rashi it is forbidden.`,
    "8#ח": `For since, etc., "but," etc. Gemara there; and what is written "from all punctures," etc.`,
    "8#ט": `Every place requiring, etc. Like safek derusah and nafalah — even b'dieved forbidden without examination, as stated in the sugya there, since a defect was born; and likewise all general punctures that are treifah without korat dam.`,
    "8#י": `Needle or thorn, etc. As stated 43b "but if found" — it implies even according to those concerned lest it healed it is treifah from one side; and as I wrote siman 33 seif 9.`,
    "8#כ": `And some are lenient, etc. — who hold that which Rabbi turned over happened thus and the beraisa is according to the plain meaning that from one side it is kosher and no examination is required.`,
    "8#ל": `And one may rely, etc. As I wrote "and some," etc.`,
  },
  "kereti": {
    "1#א": `Kivah is the meses — from the meses food enters the kivah; and this does not exist in fowl at all.`,
    "1#ב": `Chelev on the yeter — see below siman 64 where everything is explained.`,
    "11#א": `And if there is no korat dam on it — in this it appears certainly obligatory to examine after korat dam; for otherwise some are stringent to say it came from outside — and then for everyone examination is required.`,
    "11#ב": `And some are stringent unless there is recognition it came from inside — and see Peleti where I brought proof that Tosafot also permit — therefore one may lean lenient.`,
    "2#א": `Kereis punctured — and the spleen does not seal, because the spleen membrane interrupts and tame chelev.`,
    "2#ב": `Treifah — they slaughtered the animal or fowl and much water and intestinal fluid flowed — all is fine and kosher; it is weakness common in man called Wassersucht from swelling; one should not multiply treifah declarations; and so from Bach's responsum.`,
    "2#ג": `And you have nothing that seals — see Peleti where I discussed well whether chelev that Ashkenazim eat seals in communities that practice among many even to forbid it — and it is full chelev; and see settling Maharshal's difficulty and Kuntres on Peri Chadash and Sha'ar Efrayim.`,
    "2#ד": `Kosher; and if the kereis was punctured — whether to suspend that after slaughter the many opinions spread to permit, among them Peri Chadash — beautiful ruling; see below siman 50 in Peleti.`,
    "3#א": `If it tears — all this is through illness; but if through knife and the like and it reached the cavity of the body — treifah like a needle in the cavity of the body — Taz; and simple.`,
    "3#ב": `Most thickness of the flesh — this is Rambam's view; most authors disagree and we require specifically mafpolash. And see Peleti where I wrote Rambam has proof from the Gemara — see there; and I left a difficulty on Tosafot — therefore where there is no great loss, treifah according to Rambam.`,
    "3#ג": `And how much measure — this measure applies even in mafpolash — we also require this measure.`,
    "3#ד": `Most of the length — the same applies to most of the width; and the poskim mentioned one of them.`,
    "4#א": `Or in length — meaning there is deficiency; if you make a circle from it — it is like a sela; but in the previous seif it deals only with tear without deficiency — if so, even if a circle from it would not be a third of a tefach, nevertheless since its length is a tefach — treifah — Shach.`,
    "4#ב": `If it was more. See Peleti: punctures with deficiency join to a sela; punctures without deficiency join to a majority; and see what I analyzed there.`,
    "4#ג": `It will stand at a tefach — and if it is small and does not hold a tefach but only two-thirds of a tefach — ostensibly if bored like half a sela it is also treifah, for if stretched it will be a third and a half which is the majority of two-thirds of a tefach; and see Peleti — requires further study; it appears the view leans to permit — see there.`,
    "7#א": `Even, etc. — and korat dam is not required from outside for Rashi; but from inside — Shach's view requires korat dam, otherwise after slaughter it was stuck; and see Peleti where I brought supports from the Gemara — since in any case most authors disagree with Rashi, one may be lenient without korat dam from inside; and see Peleti where I challenged Rashi — and I wrote Rashi may concede regarding a needle that korat dam is required.`,
    "7#ב": `And so is the practice — Shach and Taz's view is specifically through a needle; but not through illness; even for that view which is Rashi's view we are not concerned; and see Peleti where I challenged on this; therefore in a place of loss even through illness one should be stringent.`,
    "7#ג": `If not, etc. — and if it swallowed before us and they slaughtered immediately and it is found stuck from one side in the meses — Tosafot's view at end of "With what may we light" — in this even Rashi permits; and see Peleti where I brought supports from Tosafot in Chullin — and one may rely on them.`,
    "8#א": `And korat dam found on it — specifically on it opposite the puncture from inside — so agreed most poskim; and if korat dam alone is found without puncture from inside, even if needle lies in beit hakosot — kosher — so agreed acharonim, not like Shach.`,
    "8#ב": `Kosher — and korat dam from inside does not cause treifah; and Shach reconciles that we are concerned in esophagus lest it healed — and see what I wrote siman 33 — for us there is no difference — see in Peleti.`,
    "8#ג": `A bit of outer skin — even for Rashi who forbids in the meses.`,
    "8#ד": `And this is when they did not salt. See Peleti where I brought that initially examination is certainly required after korat dam — only here it deals when not examined and salted — thereby the apparent contradiction in Tur is resolved.`,
    "8#ה": `Or it was lost — and if not examined at all to see if punctured outward — Peri Chadash's view is to be stringent; and he is correct.`,
    "8#ו": `And one may rely — and see Peleti where I proved from Tosafot that examination is always required — therefore one should be stringent.`,
    "8#ז": `Around the needle — in this even from the side of the puncture — treifah — so wrote the poskim.`,
    "8#ח": `And one may rely — and Taz is stringent; and so there are luminaries.`,
    "9#_": `Rust — meaning on the head of the needle where we doubt whether the point passed outward before or after slaughter; we say rust testifies it passed before slaughter; but in the middle of the needle rust makes no difference, for certainly it punctured and came from life; rust only perhaps did not pass the entire thickness until mafpolash — and simple; and so Maharshal.`,
  },
  "peleti": {
    "1#_": `And if the chelev, etc. — see what I wrote at length in siman 64 — see there.`,
    "11#_": `And some stringent, etc. — Tosafot imply like Rashba lenient Chullin 48b s.v. if kofa, etc. — concluded also if found inside kurkavan kosher because esophagus way needle with food kurkavan hangs on esophagus. If intent plainly needle in kurkavan — obvious all enters body; if treifah kurkavan also beit hakosot in animal treifah — not taught one side kosher what Tosafot here; rather clear: found in thickness of kurkavan swallowed in flesh — do not know inside through esophagus or outside esophagus punctured came; Tosafot like lung taught all from esophagus; kurkavan depends esophagus; inside because near esophagus; wrote kurkavan not beit hakosot — hard reality stuck without mark inside outside; since Tosafot agree to leniency — worthy to rely on them.`,
    "2#א": `Kereis, etc., and you have nothing to seal it — Taz wrote s.k. 2: that the spleen does not seal because of the spleen membrane that interrupts; and I did not understand — the chelev covering the kereis interrupts between the kereis and spleen and it is tame chelev — how does the spleen seal.`,
    "2#ב": `That it seals it — certainly for those who hold like Rabbenu Yoel; if so it is obvious fat adhering to the kereis that they eat certainly seals; and Rambam holds like Rabbenu Efrayim that it is tame chelev; and that Rama did not emend for us that fat seals — since the people of Rhineland eat it; if so they eat forbidden food, etc. — see what I wrote on this siman 64; I did not understand him; nor that Rashi wrote tame chelev does not seal because it is not attached but a resident; and Rashba in Tur haArukh 37 wrote tame chelev does not seal because there is a membrane below; and he explains "they eat" chelev of yeter — they eat forbidden chelev of yeter because it is not attached, meaning the membrane does not interrupt between it and the flesh, etc. — and from this reason the law is that it should seal even for Babylonians; and if so this fat adhering that R' Eliezer and R' Yoel fought about is the fat adhering to the kereis itself and the membrane is above it — if so what difference whether forbidden or permitted — in the end it is not attached and seals. And one may say Rambam deduced from "they eat," etc. — and for us sealing does not seal as explained. And with this Maharshal's wonder in Orach Chaim siman 54 on R' Yehudah Zahav who doubted in that siman whether it seals — and he is included in "they eat," etc. — is settled; and he wondered why according to R' Eliezer it is karet prohibition — it is only a stringency of the law like R' Akiva who requires attachment; but all agree it is not attached and certainly seals. And why wonder on "they eat" — since all Israel do not eat the kivah it would not seal even though not attached; whereas since the people of Eretz Yisrael eat it, the reason "they do not divide" does not apply; and since it is not attached — it seals. Therefore the kereis — since Rambam holds like R' Eliezer and it is forbidden as chelev and karet punishment — therefore "they eat" does not apply; unlike chelev on the yeter which is only a general stringency — we properly say "they eat," etc. And with this Peleti settles Sha'ar Efrayim and Peri Chadash who debated wisely on a needle at the head of the dekin that chelev seals the puncture whether to permit because many poskim hold "head of intestine" refers only to the end of the intestines near the stomach and it is in the category of "they eat" — see there; and why did they trouble with this — it is explained in Mordechai that "head of intestine" is not attached at all — if so obviously it seals; and what difference tame or pure — and according to my words it is fine that even if we say even though not attached it does not seal — examine well.`,
    "3#_": `Even though this tear did not reach the kereis, etc. — Rambam's view; most authors disagree as the acharonim wrote. To me: perhaps this membrane that Rashi said covers the paunch in its majority is attached to that membrane as known — if so when the thick membrane is torn, the chelev covering the kereis is attached to it — difficulty for R' Shimon who holds tame chelev seals. If so how can chelev seal the puncture — therefore Rambam holds we do not require mafpolash puncture; and sealing does not help him as above siman 46 — see Taz. Or one may say in the Gemara 52b in Samuel's matter "flesh covering most of the kereis in its majority" — Rav Ashi asked whether R' Eliezer means torn in its majority or majority removed; difficulty: open the first chapter — we establish all 18 treifot as eight punctures counted as one category entering and exiting — the Gemara asks if so punctures too, etc., but those two categories should not exit — the kereis is missing "removed" also in the mishnah; and if stolen from it impossible not to find a single one in the book; and it is not that Rashi's view is not in any way that Samuel holds like the majority — if the majority is torn from it it is revealed more than removal; and if removed from it impossible to find single — and why did he write this law unlike the spleen specifically here; one may say because he gave a general rule in siman 50, etc. — not helpful here to give measure but removal in circle or length with deficiency like sela; Tur wrote "or removed in it" — scribal error for "all of it"; Shulchan Aruch does not reconcile — he wrote first or removed meaning bored; seif 4 already wrote its law. Or our membrane is thin attached to paunch membrane — R' Shimon seals; Rambam not; sealing not helpful; see Taz. Or Gemara 52b Samuel — majority torn or majority removed; difficulty 18 treifot; missing removed in mishnah; Rashi majority torn; removal impossible find single; why unlike spleen; siman 50 rule; measure for bored; Tur error; Shulchan Aruch Rambam bored; seif 4. Or thin membrane on paunch — R' Shimon; Rambam; Taz. Gemara Rav Ashi; 18 treifot; removed; Rashi; Tur; Shulchan Aruch; thin membrane; Taz. Majority R' Yehudah amoraim; small animal majority length without tefach treifah; Bach wondered Beit Yosef proved Rambam Tur; not nothing; Samuel majority; Rif majority; Rambam Tur tefach large animal; Bach forced; majority width; Rashi necessary fowl; Rif one place; Shulchan Aruch correct Rambam; Taz correct; Peleti forced; Rosh Behag; finger below; Rosh explained; Gemara difficulty return mixed; not Rashi fowl; Rif one root finger; Shulchan Aruch stringent fowl; Rashi first necessary; Taz correct.`,
    "4#_": `If the flesh was bored, etc. — regarding gullet lacking deficiency, cracks join majority; with deficiency join sela; certainly here likewise; we do not say in treifot the derivation from gullet to skull; if cracks together majority or tefach — treifah — simple. According to Shach and Taz siman 33 drawing board long strips — why need specifically sela; even small width can cut long strips without width only length more than tefach — all difficulties on prohibition here in sela; their jeopardy there; my answer above does not sit here; truth analogy if tear like that and beside another — even in length ten tefachim — in truth "join" means lengthwise together; not beside — no difference one or two tears — not stretched more; not take beside to put in length — clear in reason. If bored like sela all lines like that each line not join more length all one stretch — truly should not join only halacha leMoshe miSinai if bored like sela and surround is tefach even not worthy to join nevertheless treifah Torah decree. Settles why "bored like sela" specifically; small animal holds tefach only two-thirds; bored half sela should be treifah stretch third and half majority two-thirds — not in Gemara; measure only sela not half; why; whole measure is kabbalah and halacha leMoshe miSinai; only tefach and majority not half sela — not treifah; examine well.`,
    "7#א": `And thus when it swallowed before us and slaughtered immediately and found stuck as Tosafot second chapter Shabbat imply — even Rashi concedes not concerned lest healed; Tosafot in Chullin in Rashi's words wrote in beit hakosot one side kosher from inside; outside needle in body cavity treifah — must understand relevance to Rashi — in beit hakosot they did not disagree; accordingly wonder even for Rashi why mishnah does not teach meses; if there no need mafpolash as above; if not about that beraisa deals stuck before us — therefore wrote not about stuck uncommon only inside; examine well.`,
    "7#ב": `Even without puncture Shach s.k. 14 wrote simply even korat dam outside not required — simple. Therefore wonder Rashi's forcing in meses not require outward because teaches needle in beit hakosot not meses; also end Shabbat chapter name changed from huvlila to beit kisei — difference needle in beit hakosot forced Rashi explain so; Tosafot difficulty mishnah meses beit hakosot punctured outward; Rabbenu Tam meses too mafpolash; Rashi outward not entirely; R' Yitzchak meses one side; esophagus; mishnah when know not healed ate needle before us; Rabbenu Tam meses mafpolash; Tur poskim Rabbenu Tam Shulchan Aruch; Rama forbid Rashi Terumat HaDeshen Orach Chaim agree; great loss lenient. Now Shach s.k. 14 not require outside; wonder Rashi forcing meses teaches beit hakosot not meses; Shabbat name change; Tosafot mishnah; Rashi; R' Yitzchak; esophagus; Rabbenu Tam; Tur; Rama; Shach. Beraisa needle beit hakosot not meses; Rashi not stuck only inside; Peleti reverse natural needle korat dam even Rashi meses require korat dam; Gemara teaches beit hakosot not meses; Tosafot difficulty; Rashi thin; Ran; Tosafot; Rashi healed; Ra'ash; Tosafot lung; no examination Ra'ash Tur hints esophagus more here; lung only blowing Ra'ash Tur 36 Beit Yosef distinguish there not blown concern puncture not recognizable; here estimation not outward no turn examine; lung thin membranes Maharam distinguish; several manners; Rambam one side kosher no examination Kessef Mishneh needle outside inside no foods liquids all agree kosher; examine.`,
    "7#ג": `Shach wrote s.k. 14 for Rashi forbid meses even without outward clearly treifah even without korat dam outside; inside Shach requires korat dam otherwise after slaughter stuck; sought Tur Choshen Mishpat not found; distinguish inside foods liquids pressed; stuck well after slaughter movement needle inside; Rashba esophagus foods liquids stream not beit hakosot rest; Shach concern after slaughter stuck; greater must say Gemara 49 why not test kofa bar or inside; Rashi kofa stuck wall mouth inside kereis certainly outside; foods liquids pressed kofa enters flesh; still hard beit hakosot kofa toward animal point toward cavity two sides why korat dam; no foods liquids pressed; you say kofa punctured before slaughter by pressure; if after slaughter all quiet no pressure; how say after slaughter; how kofa punctured came after slaughter; still question should distinguish kofa bar inside; ostensibly after slaughter by shaking animal etc. needle stuck even dead animal; Shach law correct by shaking stuck after slaughter; still initially stringent.`,
    "7#ד": `Practice Shach Taz both answer specifically needle concern healed not illness; Lechem already preceded showed all sides; to me why specifically needle; reason lest healed — all Rashi foundation teaches needle beit hakosot not meses; Ulla and supporters not concerned healed; beraisa needle beit hakosot how established meses not concerned healed; if say Ulla concedes thin frequent concern; Ra'ash challenged Rif from beit hakosot to esophagus esophagus soft unlike beit hakosot hard; forced if so why Ulla; if illness too concern healed no need soft esophagus; if illness concern healed both; punctured one without other how find; examine; therefore illness without great loss stringent.`,
    "8#א": `Kosher — see Shach s.k. 19 distinguish esophagus concern healed; wrote above siman 33 not comparable esophagus safek shechitah; more Rambam — see there.`,
    "8#ב": `Around — all agreed from one side require korat dam opposite needle; two sides not required opposite; proves korat dam alone without puncture opposite kosher; otherwise puncture from here korat dam alone would treifah; rather learn korat dam alone not treifah; so Tzitz Eliezer; correct not Shach.`,
    "8#ג": `And some lenient Taz s.k. 12 Shach s.k. 32 elaborated against each other; Taz words not obligatory; Shach difficulty Tur wrote turn over implies obligatory; above thorn esophagus Ra'avan Ra'ash proved Ulla no examination beit hakosot learn no examination; ostensibly needs answer; appears if stuck into skin how not examine — most lungs kosher; minority common need examination; here common since puncture through and only slight remnant also punctured; Tosafot compare safek derusah wrote safek derusah common since common cannot require examination all lungs need examination; here ideally certainly need only if lost without examination lung permitted also here if lost judge need examination b'dieved treifah or only stringency like lost lung kosher same here; Tur turn over wrote ideally examination like lung; above deals if inhibits when lost without examination esophagus no examination inhibits only disqualify lost lung clear; Tosafot somewhat all places need examination; else forced Tosafot Shabbat name change; not resolve simple beit hakosot second skin punctured one no examination two meses one skin need examination difference needle; always examination therefore stringent Taz Bach unless great loss; further arguments settle; if stuck body cavity beit hakosot outside inside not side to side perhaps no examination after korat dam; reverse foods liquids pressed probably common puncture whole need examination; reverse foods liquids block needle not exit outside no concern; simple in reason; no proof Rambam one side kosher no examination Rambam explains needle outside inside no foods liquids all permit; examine.`,
  },
  "pitchei-teshuva": {
    "1#_": `On the yeter. [See Be'er Heitev; and what he wrote in name of Shulchan Aruch siman 62 that wart is proof it punctured, etc. — see in Sefer Levushei Serad ot 62 and 63 who wrote if found outside a hard place in touch like a nut — requires further study, for perhaps comparable to wart; and plausible this is according to the examiner's eyes in its hardness and nature; and from Peri Megadim siman 49 s.k. 2 one may hear he holds hard flesh is not a defect — see there further; and see what I wrote s.k. 6 in name of Shevut Yaakov:]`,
    "10#_": `Forced through and kosher. Sefer HaTe'udah wrote in name of Yam Shel Shlomo: if there is like a recognizable path until outside — treifah, for it appears from life it passed; and he wrote further in name of Tur Choshen Mishpat: specifically when punctures not found immediately when removed from animal but after a day or half day — we may suspect done by man; but if immediately when removed found thus — one may not permit, for certainly done before slaughter. And he wrote on this: for us there is no difference since Rama testified we practice to declare treifah in every manner without korat dam and without rust; nevertheless I wrote for other places that do not practice like Rama, etc. — see there; and see in She'elot uTeshuvot Tolodot Yitzchak siman 5 who wrote on this: even for us there is a difference per Shach s.k. 30 in name of Bach that even punctured from both sides if no korat dam found one may permit in our time in great loss; and so one should rule; nevertheless if there is like a path outside or if immediately when removed found thus — one may not permit even in great loss — see there; and it is simple; and it appears Turei Beit Shmuel for his view who wrote in Orach Chaim 17 one forbids even in great loss punctured from both sides — unlike Shach and Peri Megadim s.k. 30 — properly there is a difference:`,
    "11#_": `No examination. Be'er Heitev; and see what I wrote above s.k. 5 and above siman 41 s.k. 11 on this matter:`,
    "12#_": `Great loss. Be'er Heitev; and what he wrote in name of Taz there is no permit from one side, etc. — see Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 64 who disagrees with Taz in what he declares treifah if stuck along length even if recognizable from inside — this is not so; Hagahot Shaarei Dura deals only when stuck in it and not recognizable inside or outside whence it came; and in this too one should not be stringent except not in great loss as Shulchan Aruch ruled in kurkavan — see there; and see Peri Chadash who also wrote per Taz it is not essential for practical law; however if stuck from outside along wall of beit hakosot — one should declare treifah — see there. Meaning: in that half-thickness of the wall from the outside side it was stuck inside the flesh along the wall and all swallowed in flesh thickness and no puncture recognizable inside or outside — treifah. Turei Beit Shmuel's view is to be more stringent: even if not stuck but lying along — treifah. Peri Megadim ruled lying — lenient; stuck — even from inside side — treifah. And see in responsum Tolodot Yitzchak siman 4 who elaborated bringing proof for Peri Chadash's view and leaned to permit in great loss; however if also found beit hakosot adhered to liver — then certainly in such a case one may not be lenient even in great loss — see there (see what I wrote above siman 41 s.k. 12 in his name); and see Noda BiYehudah Tinyana Yoreh De'ah end siman 2 who wrote on matter of needle stuck in beit hakosot and length of needle along beit hakosot — if truly stuck along length one may not be lenient except in great loss one may settle — see there:`,
    "2#_": `Kereis punctured treifah. [See in Sefer Binat Adam gate of prohibition and permission siman 26 who wrote a sharp question: they stabbed between the ribs near the belly and secretion emerged and it appeared the kereis was punctured; and afterward it lived twelve months and also became pregnant and gave birth — what is its law. He answered: ostensibly since secretion emerged, certainly the kereis was punctured — certainly treifah; and it would not help even if it lives many days as siman 57. But after examination it appears this is only safek treifah — for Tur brought view of some that inner kereis is specifically the lower part facing the ground, but above in the rib area if the kereis was punctured it is not treifah except in its majority or a tefach — and this is Ittur's view; but majority of poskim hold kereis punctured anywhere is treifah in any amount; and the annotator in Tur wrote sense and experience testify to the first view — therefore safek dispute; and since it lived twelve months or gave birth — it is kosher, end of his words — see there. In my humble opinion this permit requires further study — since majority of poskim forbid anywhere in any amount as he himself wrote — for Peri Megadim wrote siman 81 s.k. 165 safek dispute where many hold thus — even if alive twelve months one may not permit — see there. And what he brought from Tur annotator, etc. — intent seems even though majority forbid, annotator's words from sense and experience give at least balanced safek. Wonder he did not mention Bach who cried out on that annotation and wrote printers may Heaven save them insert annotation not halacha — see there. However in my humble opinion when the rabbi wished to prove kereis punctured between ribs is only safek treifah — he need not mention Ittur or annotator — seems majority who forbid anywhere in any amount do not concede obviously they hold halacha like Rabbah in chapter Elu Treifot 56b — rather from safek because halacha is doubtful like whom — see Rashi there who wrote since halacha not decided we act stringently and declare treifah entire kereis punctured in any amount; also Ra'ash there siman 33 wrote some hold halacha like Rabbah bar Rav Huna and some like Rabbach; Rashi ruled stringently like R' Yosei bar Chanina and so custom — end of his words. Presumably Tur and other poskim likewise rule stringently since safek d'oraisa — if so only safek treifah and living twelve months or birth helps per assumption safek dispute helps twelve months to permit. However on that main assumption Shach also siman 57 s.k. 48 implies so from Maharshal chapter Elu Treifot siman 80 and his words are correct; and Peri Chadash agreed s.k. 50; and so Peri Megadim and Levushei Serad and responsum Zichron Yosef Yoreh De'ah part 2 siman 2 and several acharonim — nevertheless matters require further study as Sefer Emek Halachah siman 8 wondered: if so what helps living twelve months for safek din — judged from rule whatever safek treifah such as this is kosher; and if we say no proof from living since minority live — how permit this animal; they will say two animals with defect one forbidden one permitted, etc. He concludes there for practical law not like Maharshal who holds twelve months helps safek in the act itself; not so safek in the din, etc. — end of his words; and so I found in Sefer Amudei Or newly printed siman 49 wondered on the sage in the goat incident likewise per that reasoning — if so that goat permitted us kereis puncture between ribs — why distinguish that goat or other animals since from that event the din was decided, etc. — see there. (In truth this wonder is not on the sage but on Maharshal Shach and acharonim who followed him.) Also from Bach's challenge on Tur annotator it is clear he does not hold Maharshal's view — requires further study; and see Turei Beit Shmuel siman 30 s.k. 8 and Sefer Beit Efrayim siman 35 s.k. 100:] (b) For the chelev. Be'er Heitev. And what he wrote regarding swollen chicken — see on this responsum Noda BiYehudah Tinyana Yoreh De'ah siman 25; and I brought it above siman 45. And what he wrote in name of Shevut Yaakov he heard from doctors, etc. — see there he wrote further and similarly I heard regarding chickens whose legs are swollen even the lower bone — concern comes from illness padagra common in chickens and eaters come to this illness — therefore guard your soul distance from them — end of his words:`,
    "3#_": `This tear to the kereis. See in Tiferet Moshe who wrote from this language it implies specifically torn from outside the skin and most of its thickness — not as punctured from inside most thickness while minority remains outside; and so Bach explicitly — see there; and so I ruled practically — end of his words:`,
    "4#_": `Length of the flesh. Be'er Heitev; and see in Tiferet Moshe who wrote the same in diagonal or half circle and the tear is majority or tefach without deficiency — requires further study for practical law — end of his words:`,
    "5#_": `This flesh bored in a circle. See in Tiferet Moshe who wrote in boring a small animal we estimate a third of its majority length or width as in a large animal we estimate third of tefach in boring — requires further study — see there. [And so in Sefer Pithei Teshuvah ot 10; but not view of Turei Beit Shmuel and Kereti and Peleti s.k. 5 — see there; and see Levushei Serad ot 61 who wrote from Taz siman 34 s.k. 3 appears like Pithei Teshuvah that also for majority we say reasoning if stretched; but from Tosafot Orach Chaim 4 it may be proved like Turei Beit Shmuel and poskim that wherever measure is in majority we do not apply "if stretched"; and concludes without great loss one should be stringent like Pithei Teshuvah; but with great loss one may be lenient — see there:]`,
    "6#_": `And so is the practice. [Be'er Heitev; and what he wrote however it appears we require korat dam from inside — see Turei Beit Shmuel who was stringent even without korat dam; and Peri Megadim agreed with Shach — see in responsum Chatam Sofer siman 48 what he wrote on this:]`,
    "7#_": `On it korat dam. Be'er Heitev; what he wrote but if found, etc.; and see in Sefer Nachalat Azriel Chullin daf 51 who wrote Shach comes to explain Beit Yosef's words; but according to what he wrote s.k. 31 even without stuck at all treifah if korat dam outside as Be'er Heitev s.k. 22 — all the more stuck one should forbid even not directly on it; and he wrote further one may say more stringent if not stuck at all — see there:]`,
    "8#_": `Korat dam. Be'er Heitev s.k. 3 in name of Tzitz Eliezer the same for adhesion; and so in responsum Sha'ar Efrayim siman 62 and responsum Chut haShani siman 69 and other acharonim; and see in responsum Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 64 who wrote adhesion literally in the eye we require; but if slightly swollen outside and somewhat thick and no change in flesh to defect — no treifah, for this comes from pain of needle stuck in live flesh; do not add to treifot — see there. [And see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 42 who wrote what was produced: if recognizable punctured through illness and korat dam outside — do we also say if no wound whence korat dam; this is simpler than "expression in the vessel" — thus: needle thus puncture in general; whatever korat dam opposite it teaches blow ruled there too; slight proof from above siman 40 seif 6; and so from Taz siman 37 end s.k. 3, etc.; but it appears specifically for needle we must turn initially and examine if korat dam outside — for regarding needle it is common that it healed unlike through illness recognizable did not control more. Nay: if korat dam found opposite — treifah; but examination not required for this; and this law is true — end of his words — see there:]`,
    "9#_": `And some lenient to permit. See in Degel Machaneh Efrayim who wrote to decide: where blood was inside — then certainly from life; and did not examine outside for korat dam — then treifah; but if did not examine inside or outside for korat dam and also not punctured from both sides — we say shechutah hutrah; and all the more if saw inside no korat dam and before examining outside it was lost — kosher even not in great loss. And see there it is implied from his words that even according to Shach below siman 57 s.k. 3 who ruled where no great loss even in safek that did not leave presumption from life one forbids — nevertheless here kosher even not in great loss, for there is double safek perhaps after slaughter perhaps not punctured outward — see there:`,
  },
  "nekudot-hakesef": {
    "1#_": `(Siman 48 in Taz s.k. 5) He wrote on Beit Yosef that his words are very astonishing and he elaborated and did not get to the end of his mind — for even though Beit Yosef's language is ambiguous, nevertheless it is simple his intent is specifically tear by knife is in any amount; but not by illness — only he was precise that it deals with not mafpolash since it also deals with knife; and so explicitly in Kesef Mishneh; one examining Kesef Mishneh will see deficiency in Beit Yosef's language; and intent of his words as in Darkei Moshe — see there.`,
    "2#_": `(There s.k. 6) His words are not clear — examine well.`,
    "3#_": `(Taz s.k. 12) His words are astonishing — for Beit Yosef explains Rabbi turned it over by chance; if so Abaye spoke well that he troubled for nothing since this young scholar did not come to teach we must turn it over; also what he wrote regarding Rambam's view is not clear — for Rambam never mentioned law of one side; also what he wrote regarding Ra'ash is certainly not so as Shach s.k. 32 wrote; also what he brought from Rashba's responsum siman 360 — one may say it deals with puncture from two sides; but from one side one may say he holds no examination is required even ideally; and so Maharshal Orach Chaim siman 91 and responsum Ben Lev kelal 9 responsum 3 and Beit Chadash and other acharonim like Rabbi Rama; and so we rule.`,
    "4#_": `(There s.k. 13) And in my humble opinion, etc. — Maharoch and Bach already preceded him in this as Shach s.k. 26 wrote.`,
    "5#_": `(There Taz 17) He copied Darkei Moshe's words and wondered on Beit Chadash; it was hidden from him all Shach wrote siman 49 s.k. 4 that Beit Chadash's words are clear and Darkei Moshe's astonishing; see there. Also what he wrote practice every day declare treifah punctured from both sides even without korat dam outside — no proof from practice, for even in beit hakosot practice is thus because we are not expert in korat dam; and further what they declare treifah is when there is rust which is like korat dam.`,
    "6#_": `(There end s.k. 18) His words are not compelling.`,
    "7#_": `(There s.k. 19) He challenged Maharam for not according to law — for Maharam himself concludes end of that responsum: one may not rely on a gentile since it was stuck in a manner that was not punctured outward, for we do not rely on gentile words neither to forbid nor to permit; and you would say if so we need not believe the gentile that he found the needle stuck, etc. — this is not so, etc., until: if so in our case too — since an Israelite came while the gentile was still dealing with removing the innards from the kereis and found the nail in the gentile's hand with legs to speak — gentile's words are only like revealing a matter in the world; but if after the gentile stopped dealing with removing innards and already removed the kereis from his hand and came to the Jew with this iron nail in hand and said he found it stuck in the kereis — we need not believe him and I have no power to forbid — end of his words. If so all he brought to contradict Maharam is irrelevant here — only general revelation. However requires further study why Maharam concludes there "and I have no power to forbid" except if the Jew was silent — this depends on disputes whether one witness is believed in prohibitions — not to elaborate now. And what is one witness believed in prohibitions regarding a gentile — even for a robber we rule even silent not believed as I wrote siman 127 Shach s.k. 9; all the more a gentile; and it must be Maharam does not refer to the gentile but generally; and because of his method he mentioned the gentile. He further challenged Maharam regarding water in brain not according to law — for Maharam himself wrote there the reason we practice to declare treifah in brain is because it is common; and not as Darkei Moshe's reason the defect was from life; and he did not mention common reason at all; and this is the main reason; and so in hagahah siman 50 he did not mention common reason; and what requires examination is no proof it is common for prohibition; and also what the rabbi wrote there in hagahah even though it is treifah that has examinations — implies all the more treifah without examination — law is thus; and see Shach siman 50 chapter 6 at length on this.`,
  },
  "kaf-hachayim": {
    "1#_": `(1) [Seif 1] Kivah punctured — treifah; and if chelev on the yeter seals the puncture — kosher; but on the arch does not seal. Explanation: kivah shaped like arch; chelev on it outside called dakashta; inside circle called yeter — chelev in extra place on arch. Even though we rule siman 64 seif 14 chelev on yeter forbidden and tame chelev does not seal — reason here it seals because Gemara says people of Eretz Yisrael eat chelev on yeter; people of Bavel practiced forbid; nevertheless helps also Babylonians who permit through sealing puncture; but on arch forbidden to eat for everyone — therefore does not seal. Taz s.k. 1; from Gemara and Beit Yosef; see below ot 7.`,
    "10#_": `(10) A goat stabbed between ribs near belly; secretion emerged; appeared kereis punctured; afterward lived twelve months; also impregnated and gave birth — Binat Adam ot 26; on Chochmat Adam kelal 30 to permit; wrote reason since safek dispute — twelve months and birth help as Shach siman 57 s.k. 48 — see there. However Machatzit HaShekel there rejected his words — erred; Shach Rashba and Bach did not write except in dispute of Ittur; also already disputed; we do not rely; but here dispute of amoraim in Gemara — how can one permit; see there. And so Chok L'Yisrael Yoreh De'ah part 1 siman 25 daf 34 — between dispute in Gemara and unresolved question in Gemara — twelve months pregnancy and birth do not help; but in dispute of poskim after sealing Talmud — living twelve months and birth help — see there. Beit Din Zivchei Tzedek ot 6.`,
    "11#_": `(11) There: and it has nothing to seal, etc. — part of kereis called kalyo in foreign tongue; that place is constricted; if punctured in that constricted place even if no pure chelev to seal — kosher as when punctured in attachment of meses and beit hakosot seif 7 — kosher because puncture not mafpolash outward. Sha'arei De'ah ot 4; Zivchei Tzedek ot 7.`,
    "12#_": `(12) Chelev adhering to kereis — even though people of Rhineland eat it like Rabbenu Yoel — for us who do not eat it does not seal. Beit Levi ot 4; Machatzit HaShekel ot 6; Chochmat Adam kelal 30 ot 2; Zivchei Tzedek ot 8; and implied but for Rhineland who eat it seals; and so Beit Levi there; see below siman 64 seif 9 and our words there in s.d.`,
    "2#_": `(2) Chelev on the arch — if safek arose one should be stringent for forbidden to everyone; first version is primary. Turei Beit Shmuel ot 2; She'arim ot 1; Machatzit HaShekel ot 1; Zivchei Tzedek ot 1.`,
    "3#_": `(3) And if we find a place that practiced leniency in dakashta for eating or even only for sealing — one may nullify their custom. Turei Beit Shmuel there; Machatzit HaShekel ot 3; Zivchei Tzedek ot 2; Ani Tzaddik there.`,
    "4#_": `(4) Chelev of yeter — now practiced everywhere to forbid eating; one may not be lenient for them. Beit Yosef siman 64 in name of Mahari Chaviv; Shulchan Aruch there seif 14. Machatzit HaShekel ot 4.`,
    "5#_": `(5) This specifically for Sephardim and Ashkenazim generally; but a place that practiced per Rambam's composition such as Yemen cities — or if you find a community that came from Sephardi cities and they and ancestors practiced permit yeter per Rambam and accepted so was practice in Spain — they practice their custom to permit. Machatzit HaShekel ot 5.`,
    "6#_": `(6) For majority of the world who forbid yeter — if safek arose one may permit for it is only custom and derabbanan — stringency ancestors practiced; one must practice like them. Machatzit HaShekel ot 6.`,
    "7#_": `(7) Chelev of yeter — we rule tame does not seal; there is dispute between Rashi and Rambam; per Maran Beit Yosef siman 64: for Rashi chelev outside kivah is dakashta; inside circle yeter — place of yeter meaning chelev of arch; for Rambam it is a drawn string like extra; Shach and Peri Chadash followed below siman 64 — implied from their words there is dispute; Turei Beit Shmuel concerned; wrote when such comes before rabbi must be careful sealing chelev be yeter for everyone; Maharsham ot 1 wrote therefore fallen this warning in Bira — who knows today which permitted string; and it appears in great loss all chelev from inside one may be lenient. Machatzit HaShekel ot 10; Etz Chaim there ot 3; but Taz siman 63 ot 6 wrote per our minority and knowledge we may not rely at all; worthy to forbid puncture in kivah whether from inside or outside; sealing chelev does not help at all.`,
    "8#_": `(8) [Seif 2] Kereis punctured, etc. — meaning all the kereis; also saniya dibi; also where there is no word — all mentioned are in category kereis; treifah punctured in any amount. Turei Beit Shmuel end ot 4; Machatzit HaShekel ot 3; Zivchei Tzedek ot 4.`,
    "9#_": `(9) Indeed there is a distinction: if puncture in kereis below toward ground — certainly treifah; but if puncture hidden under ribs on this side and that — safek treifah. Chochmat Adam kelal 20 ot 2; Machatzit HaShekel there; Zivchei Tzedek ot 5.`,
  },
  "mateh-yehonatan": {
    "1#_": `(Siman 48 seif 2) Kereis punctured, etc. See Taz s.k. 2; they challenge why spleen should not seal like chelev on yeter — we rule like Babylonians who forbid eating chelev on yeter; nevertheless for sealing it seals as Rabbenu Nissim said "they eat" and we seal; not astonishing; if so same in kereis since Rhineland eat chelev with it — for us it should seal. They answer: granted chelev on yeter is permitted by law — halacha like R' Akiva requires attachment of membrane and peeling; chelev on yeter is not attachment but resident; Babylonians forbid from stringency — one may say "they eat," etc.; unlike chelev on kereis which we forbid — we rule like Rabbenu Efrayim; for us it is full chelev and karet liable — no language to say "they eat." Only on what Rama wrote below siman 67 seif 9: but we do not forbid vessels of Rhineland — therefore reason as Maharil in collection: that generation agreed not to forbid vessels because for essential law it appeared to rule like Rabbenu Yoel; only on chelev itself they did not wish to permit from chelev stringency; if so still difficult why not seal like chelev on yeter; and one must say: here regarding kereis since from stringency they accepted Rabbenu Efrayim's view — for us it is d'oraisa chelev with karet — therefore no "they eat"; unlike chelev on yeter which is only general stringency — we properly say "they eat," etc.`,
    "2#_": `(There seif 4) If the flesh was bored, etc., that if stretched, etc. — so Ra'ash: if bored like sela — treifah, for if stretched will stand at tefach — meaning thread surrounding the bore when stretched will be tefach; width of sela is third of tefach — end of his words. One may analyze: in bored like sela there is not a tefach length of tear — nevertheless treifah because thread surrounding bore holds tefach; if so why need deficiency of sela — even in less would be treifah if one could make one strip length tefach from the bore — for from bore one can make many strips length tefach. Rashi Orach Chaim 50b s.v. she'im timtach: if stretched here and there so width enters — length or width you find tefach in that bore — end; appears he explains width of sela is two-thirds tefach and circumference two tefachim; when stretched in length tear length tefach; if so according to his words — for us every tear without length tefach kosher; we learn nothing from thread surrounding bore — unlike Ra'ash difficulty. And that which Gemara says Rabbi Asi bored like sela treifah if stretched stands tefach; R' Chiya: for me they explained sela kosher more than like sela treifah; and mishnah taught measure in tefach — if so bored like sela treifah; must say they dispute whether sela is third tefach treifah or less than third kosher — great forcedness they dispute reality — in their times sela coins common to remove doubt. Also Shach's difficulty above siman 41 s.k. 5 calculation not precise per geometers; must say Ra'ash holds measure bored like sela treifah not because if stretched will be tefach and forbidden for tefach measure — rather treifah in itself; halacha leMoshe miSinai if bored like sela treifah even without tear length tefach. Therefore no wonder they dispute reality — all agree sela is third tefach; they dispute where halacha leMoshe miSinai applies. That which teaches "if stretched," etc. — not because treifah reason is thus; only mentions per current statement "now that bored" — halacha leMoshe miSinai; certainly if multiple punctures with much deficiency until among all like sela — also treifah like gullet and elsewhere; therefore needed to say if stretched, etc. — to teach punctures must be in one place specifically; need tefach in one place like tear — certainly here little here little do not join; same several places not one place not treifah. Therefore also Shach's difficulty resolved — bored like sela is halacha leMoshe miSinai in truth; not from tear reason; he did not care to analyze calculation; mentioned tefach though not precise; examine well.`,
    "3#_": `(There seif 7 in hagahah) And some forbid, etc. Difference between meses and beit hakosot per Tosafot for Rashi's view: beit hakosot thick — no concern lest healed; unlike meses thin — more concern. Ra'ash in sugya thorn stuck esophagus: Ra'avan ruled like Ulla; brought proof from needle in thickness beit hakosot; possible Rav Alfas distinguishes — thickness beit hakosot thick not concerned lest healed; plausible like Ra'avan — no distinction for any thickness; Tur in hints wrote Ra'ash holds like Rashi in meses puncture one side treifah; if so must distinguish thick and thin; Ra'ash certainly does not distinguish thick thin — difference meses beit hakosot: beit hakosot hard wall; meses soft wall — therefore one side also treifah. Still difficult: what proof from beit hakosot to esophagus; Taz view and Rashi implies even puncture one side through illness treifah in meses; unlike esophagus illness kosher; otherwise punctured one without other how find. From beginning Orach Chaim slight proof for Shach's view meses not forbidden one side only through thorn not illness — Gemara answers meses and beit hakosot counted as one; if so difficult for Rashi meses not equal beit hakosot in puncture — meses even one side treifah; beit hakosot must be two sides; reason there treifah one side concern lest healed; we say certainly punctured two sides and healed; if so equal beit hakosot.`,
    "4#_": `(In Shach s.k. 17) And implied in meses treifah even without korat dam outside, etc. — but from inside must be korat dam as Shach s.k. 30; ostensibly not compelled; one may say: granted beit hakosot no concern lest healed — specifically korat dam required; but in meses concern lest healed — we say there was korat dam and korat dam also returned and healed; further distinguish: granted beit hakosot korat dam required outside — for although certainly stuck from life, nevertheless without korat dam outside we say stuck from inside but not punctured outward until after death; but this we certainly do not say it stuck after death inside. If so in meses since certainly stuck from life inside — treifah even without korat dam at all — for even punctured one side treifah; but Ran and Rashba prove like Shach who challenged esophagus why thorn stuck treifah even without korat dam — why not say after slaughter punctured like beit hakosot; answered since esophagus narrow place foods liquids pass frequently — we say foods liquids passed and impossible here concern lest healed — we also say korat dam afterward healed and recovered — no difference; for in esophagus too concern lest healed; and certainly descended from life; if so korat dam neither raises nor lowers; therefore no distinction as above; also in meses korat dam required from inside.`,
    "5#_": `(There in Shulchan Aruch seif 8) If found in one needle, etc. — so Tur's language; Bach challenged: from Ra'ash proves no examination required — Ra'ash explains thorn esophagus what is written there and regarding safek derusah requires examination — see there; afterward brought Ra'avan who ruled like Ulla therefore no examination required; brings proof from needle in thickness beit hakosot; if so proves no need to turn over; answers: certainly ideally must turn over; but if salted rinsed or lost — permitted; same esophagus ideally requires examination; brings proof needle beit hakosot — see there; see siman 50 in hagahah; you will find opposite his words.`,
    "6#_": `(In Shulchan Aruch) And this is when they did not salt or rinse. See Shach s.k. 23 what he wrote in my humble opinion Rashba also concedes, etc. — this is not so; Rashba follows his view: where defect may come and no place to suspend — we do not establish on presumption; see below siman 61 what he wrote.`,
    "7#_": `(There in hagahah) And some lenient, etc., and one may rely, etc. Shach s.k. 33; here he brought Ra'ash and Rambam's words; ostensibly not precise: granted in matter stated below there is distinction expert and not expert; not so in matter lost — reason since nothing to rely on defect we establish presumption of permissibility; if so for us too even not expert; and must be that which mentions "or lost" means needle and beit hakosot lost — stated below; unknown whether punctured outward; depends on expertise — perhaps if expert puncture before us; properly required Rambam Ra'ash view no examination required — kosher even for us.`,
    "8#_": `(In Shulchan Aruch seif 11) Needle found in kurkavan, etc., suspend lenient. Because esophagus swallows; food enters through there — one may say IRC double safek inside or outside; if outside safek punctured inner organs; Shach siman 45 s.k. 5; must say because double safek examination required after korat dam; unlike reason esophagus swallows — no examination outside; those stringent forbid even in such case — not permit from double safek; one may say because found in kurkavan flesh we say because esophagus swallows we say entered through esophagus punctured esophagus exited body cavity came kurkavan outside — forced.`,
  },
  "yad-avraham": {
    "1#_": `(Siman 48 seif 4) If this flesh was bored in circle or length if more than sela — treifah if this tear stretched will stand tefach. Appears to learn: in large animal tear measure tefach; in small animal measure is majority which is less than tefach; same in boring — if stretch tear will stand at its majority less than sela — examine well. However I saw Turei Beit Shmuel not so; see Peri Megadim and Lechem. And know: per Rambam and Shulchan Aruch previous seif if majority thickness torn — like mafpolash; same regarding bored; so clear from Rashba Tur haArukh (43) — see there; examine well. Peri Megadim simply; wondered on Shach s.k. 7. However Shach followed his long book that wrote per Rambam bored means mafpolash; if not mafpolash not bored; we estimate length width circle like sela — see there. Peri Megadim challenged: Gemara says if stretched stands tefach — implies because of tefach; if so even not mafpolash; no proof — nevertheless lenient from tefach measure in small animal as Turei Beit Shmuel wrote. Thus bored in circle no majority in length or width alone lenient; for this reason lenient also not mafpolash — only helps in mafpolash since no majority on one side only in circle. And in my humble opinion bring proof for Rambam — resolves Tosafot Orach Chaim 43 s.v. henakh, etc. — majority outer torn and flesh covering also counted as one; so Turei Beit Shmuel. What wondered on Beit Yosef — Rambam holds puncture to cavity requires examination — Shach already preceded in long book.`,
    "2#_": `(Seif 8) And if the needle passed from side to side while still stuck, etc., they examine; if no korat dam found after slaughter forced through kosher if not salted; but if salted — treifah even without korat dam — lest salt removed it. In my humble opinion learned from Avodah Zarah 32b; Rashi, Rif, Ra'ash, Ran, Semak regarding Lebuvin hides — round tear opposite heart forbidden in benefit; we say in Gemara and those poskim: if korat dam on it — then certainly from life; if no korat dam — we say after slaughter torn; and there: and when not salted; but salted — say salt removed prohibition even without korat dam. Clear proof for Shulchan Aruch. Not like Hagahot Asheri chapter 1 doubtful in this law if rinsed or salted whether we say shechutah hutrah — see there. Not so — we do not say thus unless there is reason to suspend it happened after slaughter as hagahah siman 50. Do not challenge Lebuvin hides: he said if korat dam — if no korat dam we say after slaughter; Gemara Orach Chaim 51 all general punctures where nothing to draw blood like needle thorn in puncture — no korat dam required; possible from life yet no korat dam — wiped by moisture of flesh with nothing to draw; one may say blood wiped by flesh moisture as Rashi there — see there; unlike hides blood can cling in hair; since no korat dam and not salted — certainly after slaughter; and who omitted this in Hilchot Avodah Zarah chapter 7 — appears because of difficulty above and holds that sugya pushed aside Chullin sugya we rule where nothing to draw no korat dam required; settles Lechem Mishneh's wonder there.`,
    "3#_": `(Seif 11) Needle found in kurkavan all swallowed in flesh thickness not recognizable any side — suspend lenient, etc. Hagahah: some stringent; concern their words if not great loss. Appears simple: if kofa inward — toward kurkavan side; point toward cavity — kosher for in kofa cannot puncture if kofa like date pit as siman 41 seif 6; so Ra'ash Orach Chaim regarding needle found in thigh thickness; Tur Yoreh De'ah siman 91 seif 3 implies explicitly when not recognizable any side; nevertheless if kofa outward — kosher; I would have said forbid kofa toward cavity; on this he gave reason its way to scrape wall; even kofa toward cavity enters — see there; but kofa outward without that — kosher.`,
  },
  "yad-ephraim": {
    "1#_": `And it has nothing to seal it for the chelev. Be'er Heitev; see above siman 46 s.k. 3 where I wrote Beit Yosef's responsum and other responsa on this; and what he wrote regarding swollen chicken — see below siman 50; regarding worms see what I wrote in Binat Adam siman 36 and pamphlet of responsa there.`,
    "2#_": `But since most of the thickness was torn. Be'er Heitev; see in Chinuch Beit Yosef siman 40 — woman dealing with kereis to clean; while dragging secretion and passing knife saw needle crossed with secretion; see Radbaz part 2 siman 227 kereis needle stuck not visible inside; see Maharam Alashkar flesh covering majority kereis torn or bored; Shulchan Aruch responsa part 8 siman 12 round puncture between ribs — no concern derusah in round puncture as Zevachim chapter of mixtures; and all that can be suspended to illness we suspend; if cannot suspend to illness such as flesh around puncture healthy not punctured as illness way; also no concern lest punctured by thorn — thin puncture impossible to examine thin puncture — for you said puncture large; many times came before me told gored by horn and I permitted after examination; see Pri Tevuah siman 40 protrusion in animal from goring oxen.`,
    "3#_": `And so is the practice. See Be'er Heitev; Noda BiYehudah Tinyana siman 69 wrote if adhesion there and not adhesion exactly opposite stuck needle inside beit hakosot — do not forbid if needle in thick place; but in thin place since even without adhesion cannot permit except in great loss — if adhesion even if needle not stuck exactly opposite — do not be lenient even in great loss; if time of pressure also near Shabbat and Yom Tov — one may settle the matter — see there; see Mordechai seif 22 needle stuck in skin beit hakosot and from there adhered to liver courtyard.`,
    "4#_": `Needle found in cavity of body — treifah. Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah part 2 siman 64 beit hakosot — needle stuck along wall; rabbi immediately declared treifah; answered if lying within skin like bolt not recognizable whence came — stringent not in great loss; but usually coarse case has great loss and may permit if needle not recognizable from side to side and korat dam outside or wart or adhered elsewhere as Kaf HaChayim kelal 89; requires further study; he is Tzitz Eliezer siman 49; nevertheless adhesion literally in eye we require; but if slightly swollen outside and no change in flesh to defect — no treifah — comes from pain of needle stuck in live flesh; do not add to treifot; but korat dam outside or adhesion and similar outside — needle found even if not stuck — declare treifah lest punctured and healed; in great loss rely on Tzitz Eliezer and Peri Chadash who are lenient also in this — see there he wrote open rebuke on rabbi who immediately declared treifah though had basis to rely; nevertheless Torah protects Israel's money; should be moderate in ruling; sages said student may not rule where his rabbi is unless three parsa away; all the more our rabbis are the books spread among Israel — whoever reaches ruling should not rule any ruling without first examining book; so I received from my teachers of blessed memory; especially I heard so from my master the famous Gaon Maharsham who sat on ruling chair in Prague community forty years; nevertheless did not rule without examining book first in Be'er Heitev; and relied on matter then not ashamed to look, etc.; and bar min dan worthy to do so where they are not Torah scholars and will not suspect them of stringency not according to law from piety and similar; he is not concerned about loss of their money — for he ruled thus without examining book, etc.; and this is not stealing people's minds but "you shall be clean," etc. — see there.`,
    "5#_": `If he is sharp. See Be'er Heitev; Noda BiYehudah Tinyana siman 30 — gentile butcher; Jewish slaughterer with him; sold innards to gentile and Jew saw removed from meses one nail that was above; unknown if in place where they pour into each other; butcher gentile whose way to mark which animal slaughtered first says sold to gentile is from first animal — forbid first and permit others; see responsum Chinuch Beit Yosef siman 91 — Israel sold kereis with innards to gentile; gentile came said found needle stuck in kereis.`,
  },
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd": {
    "1#_": `(Siman 48 Shach s.k. 19) That it is at rest forever. Accordingly when needle not found stuck in it — concern lest punctured outward and healed — requires further study for practical law.`,
    "2#_": `(Seif 8 in hagahah) If it is great loss. Conclusion of acharonim: needle found stuck in beit hakosot from one side and opposite outside korat dam; likewise stuck from both sides across even without korat dam — treifah even in great loss. Found stuck one side examined no korat dam outside — kosher even not in great loss. If lost without examination or rinsed — no need to examine. Likewise needle lying in beit hakosot korat dam found outside opposite needle — kosher in great loss. In meses and in beit hakosot thin place stuck one side korat dam inside — kosher only in great loss. No korat dam at all — kosher without great loss. [And rinsed from inside impossible examine and examined outside no korat dam — requires further study.] Rust or adhesion — law like korat dam. Not called punctured one side unless examined not from side to side; but without examination even if by touch felt needle does not emerge outside — nevertheless law as punctured two sides; nevertheless in such case Bach lenient in great loss — one may rely on Bach to be lenient.`,
    "3#_": `(Taz s.k. 19) And this is with mesalleles. In Menachem Tziyon kelal 50 s.k. 7 wrote these matters contradict what Taz wrote below (siman 122 s.k. 4) end that believed even without mesalleles forbidden; and so Shach above (siman 47 s.k. 24); and so is primary.`,
  },
};
